WEBVTT

00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:10.470
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. I think the thing that gets to me the most about the story that we're doing today is I feel for the parents in this story.
<v Ira Glass>来自芝加哥WBEZ，这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。我觉得我们今天报道的这个故事最触动我的地方在于，我非常同情故事里的父母。

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:27.440
<v Ira Glass>Like when you're a parent, at some point you're in the situation where your kid is having experiences and friendships with people that are completely out of your reach, right? You don't know what's really happening between them. You don't really know what's going through your kid's head. You're powerless.
<v Ira Glass>作为父母，在某个阶段，你的孩子会拥有一些你完全无法触及的经历和人际关系，对吧？你不知道他们之间到底发生了什么。你真的不知道孩子脑子里在想什么。你感到无能为力。

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:43.070
<v Ira Glass>The parents in the story today, they thought they were close to their son, who was doing really well in school. Things seemed fine. Anyway, it's our whole hour today, a story we call Chip in My Brain. David Kestenbaum tells what happened, which begins with basketball.
<v Ira Glass>今天故事里的父母，他们以为自己很了解儿子，儿子在学校表现也很出色。一切看起来都很正常。总之，这是我们今天整整一小时的节目，故事名为《我大脑里的芯片》。大卫·凯斯滕鲍姆（David Kestenbaum）将讲述事情的经过，故事始于篮球。

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:54.780
<v David Kestenbaum>It would probably be wrong to say that Cody Treybig loved basketball at the beginning. It would definitely be wrong to say he was good at it. He wasn't at all. He was good at World of Warcraft though, the video game.
<v David Kestenbaum>说科迪·特雷比格（Cody Treybig）一开始就热爱篮球，可能不太准确。说他擅长篮球就更不对了。他一点也不擅长。但他很擅长玩电子游戏《魔兽世界》。

00:00:54.780 --> 00:01:10.030
<v Cody Treybig>Sometimes I still almost cry thinking about the first time I walked into Stormwind, the capital human city. And the music changes. And you see this beautiful, huge castle. And you're walking in, and it's like-- it's just really-- it's amazing.
<v Cody Treybig>有时想到第一次走进人类主城暴风城的情景，我还是会热泪盈眶。音乐变了，你看到那座美丽而巨大的城堡。你走进去，感觉就像——这真的——太神奇了。

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:23.880
<v David Kestenbaum>Cody says he wasn't exactly avoiding physical activity. The game was just super fun. He and his friends would all log on, go on adventures together. As someone who used to play a lot of video games, it felt like bad etiquette not to ask who his online character was.
<v David Kestenbaum>科迪说他并不是在刻意回避体育锻炼。只是游戏太好玩了。他和朋友们一起登录，一起去冒险。作为一个曾经玩过很多电子游戏的人，我觉得如果不问问他的游戏角色是谁，显得很不礼貌。

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:37.980
<v David Kestenbaum>Cody told me-- and knowing him a little bit now, this seems entirely appropriate-- he was a paladin, which is a holy knight who can fight. But also, he's nice. He casts healing spells when his co-adventurers get hurt. Cody eventually built them up to level 60, as high as you can go.
<v David Kestenbaum>科迪告诉我——现在稍微了解他一点之后，觉得这完全符合他的性格——他玩的是圣骑士，一个能战斗的圣骑士。而且他人很好。当队友受伤时，他会施放治疗法术。科迪最终把他们练到了60级，那是当时的满级。

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:42.840
<v Cody Treybig>I was just really proud of having six-eighths of Lightforge gear, which was nothing. But--
<v Cody Treybig>我当时特别自豪，因为我凑齐了六分之八的“光铸”装备，虽然那其实算不了什么。但是——

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:59.760
<v David Kestenbaum>All of which is just to say that sports were not Cody's top priority. In gym class when they'd have to run laps, he'd always be at the back. Basketball somehow though, he kind of liked. His dad had played when he was growing up. So early on, in third grade, Cody found himself on this local youth league team.
<v David Kestenbaum>说这一切只是想表达，运动并不是科迪的首要任务。体育课上要跑圈时，他总是落在最后。不过不知为何，他还是挺喜欢篮球的。他爸爸小时候打过球。所以早在他三年级的时候，科迪就参加了当地的青年联盟球队。

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:09.760
<v Cody Treybig>I was not very athletic at all. I remember being at basketball games with my friend Daniel. And we'd be talking about World of Warcraft while the game was going on on the bench.
<v Cody Treybig>我那时一点也不运动。我记得和朋友丹尼尔一起参加篮球赛时，我们在替补席上打球的同时，却在讨论《魔兽世界》。

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:13.700
<v Drew Treybig>OK. Well, from Mom's perspective, I thought he was adorable.
<v Drew Treybig>好吧。从妈妈的角度来看，我觉得他很可爱。

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:17.520
<v David Kestenbaum>That's Mom, Drew Treybig. And here's Dad, Jimmy Treybig.
<v David Kestenbaum>那是妈妈，德鲁·特雷比格（Drew Treybig）。这是爸爸，吉米·特雷比格（Jimmy Treybig）。

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:19.620
<v Jimmy Treybig>He wasn't real aggressive.
<v Jimmy Treybig>他那时并不怎么好斗。

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:22.770
<v Drew Treybig>He's in the middle of the court, watching everybody run back and forth.
<v Drew Treybig>他站在球场中央，看着大家跑来跑去。

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:26.720
<v Jimmy Treybig>And if he got the ball and someone wanted it, he'd just give it to them.
<v Jimmy Treybig>如果他拿到球，有人想要，他就会直接给他们。

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:45.540
<v Drew Treybig>He would just so sweetly hand it off to another player. I didn't really have a problem with that, to be honest, at that age. He was little. I thought, this is my dear, sweet boy. And he'll get it. He'll get it eventually. But it bothered him, after a while, that the boys were making fun of him.
<v Drew Treybig>他会那么甜美地把球交给另一个球员。老实说，在那个年纪，我并不觉得这有什么问题。他还小。我想，这是我亲爱、甜美的孩子。他会明白的。他终究会明白的。但过了一段时间，男孩们嘲笑他，这让他感到很困扰。

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:56.190
<v David Kestenbaum>Drew describes herself as a kind of helicopter mom, which seems right. While I was there talking to Cody, she kept momming us. Are you guys hungry? How about a smoothie? I'm going to get one.
<v David Kestenbaum>德鲁形容自己是一个“直升机妈妈”，这形容很贴切。当我在那儿和科迪交谈时，她一直在照顾我们。“你们饿吗？来杯冰沙怎么样？我去买一杯。”

00:02:56.190 --> 00:03:11.940
<v David Kestenbaum>Drew says the whole family is kind of kooky. Whenever someone was leaving, they'd be sure to say I love you, to the other person. And I don't mean leaving the house, just like leaving the room. Cody's dad, Jimmy, says he had a hard time getting his father to say I love you. So this is their fix.
<v David Kestenbaum>德鲁说全家人都有点古怪。每当有人要离开时，他们一定会对彼此说“我爱你”。我指的不是离开家，只是离开房间。科迪的爸爸吉米说，他很难让他自己的父亲说出“我爱你”。所以这是他们的解决办法。

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:28.170
<v David Kestenbaum>The Treybigs live in Texas in the outskirts of Austin. Jimmy was an early tech entrepreneur. He founded a company that made the computers that ran ATM machines. So they had a nice house on a big piece of land. There were cows around, but no neighbors you could walk to, which Jimmy told me he regretted.
<v David Kestenbaum>特雷比格一家住在德克萨斯州奥斯汀郊外。吉米是一位早期的科技创业者。他创办了一家制造ATM机电脑的公司。所以他们在一大片土地上拥有一个不错的房子。周围有牛，但没有可以步行拜访的邻居，吉米告诉我他对此感到遗憾。

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:43.380
<v David Kestenbaum>As it happened, there was an old basketball court out behind the house. So they decided to get Cody some lessons. Like instead of a piano teacher or something, get him a basketball teacher. His dad found someone through the local youth league, a guy named AJ. Cody is in third grade at the time.
<v David Kestenbaum>碰巧的是，房子后面有一个旧的篮球场。所以他们决定给科迪请教练。就像给孩子请钢琴老师一样，给他请个篮球老师。他爸爸通过当地青年联盟找到了一个人，一个叫AJ的家伙。当时科迪在上三年级。

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:52.670
<v Cody Treybig>So I remember the first meeting. He came in the front door. And he was just very friendly, is what I remember feeling at the time, tall, 6 foot 6".
<v Cody Treybig>我记得第一次见面。他从前门进来。我记得当时的感觉是他非常友好，个子很高，有6英尺6英寸。

00:03:52.670 --> 00:04:19.780
<v Cody Treybig>And I think I probably asked him, oh my god, can you dunk? And he's like, oh yeah, I can dunk. He said they used to call him "Springs" was his nickname because he dunked a lot.
<v Cody Treybig>我想我当时可能问他：“天哪，你能扣篮吗？”他说：“哦，是的，我能扣篮。”他说以前别人叫他“弹簧”，因为他扣篮很多。

00:04:19.780 --> 00:04:33.090
<v David Kestenbaum>So that first day, AJ walks in. They all hang out by the front door. Cody is shy, but AJ gets right down on his level. The two of them go out to the basketball court, talking the whole way. When they get there, Cody can't really even do a layup.
<v David Kestenbaum>那天AJ进门时，他们都在前门附近。科迪很害羞，但AJ很快和他打成一片。两人一路聊天走到篮球场。到了那儿，科迪甚至连上篮都不会。

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:46.510
<v David Kestenbaum>And AJ is like, OK, we can fix this. Let's do some basics. He teaches Cody this drill where you stand just to the side of the basket, try to bank it in off this magic spot. Then you get the rebound, go to the other side, and do it again, back and forth, back and forth.
<v David Kestenbaum>AJ说：“好吧，我们可以解决这个问题。让我们做些基础训练。”他教科迪一个练习：站在篮筐侧面，尝试利用那个“魔法点”打板进球。然后抢篮板，到另一边，再做一次，来来回回。

00:04:46.510 --> 00:05:00.820
<v Cody Treybig>And he would put his hand through the hoop and act like a little monster, a dragon or something, squiggle his fingers around like, feed the monster. Feed the monster. [CHUCKLES] So I thought that was pretty fun.
<v Cody Treybig>他会把手伸过篮筐，像个小怪物，或者龙之类的东西，手指扭动着说：“喂食小怪兽。喂食小怪兽。”（轻笑）我觉得那挺好玩的。

00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:12.750
<v David Kestenbaum>AJ works with Cody two days a week, then three days a week. Sometimes other kids come over so they can all train together. And it's great. Mom would bring out snacks for everyone. Or if other kids weren't around, Mom or Dad would get drafted.
<v David Kestenbaum>AJ每周和科迪训练两天，后来是三天。有时其他孩子也会过来，他们可以一起训练。这很棒。妈妈会给每个人拿零食。如果其他孩子不在，妈妈或爸爸会被拉来陪练。

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:25.470
<v Drew Treybig>We would stand there and pretend to be a player on the other team so that they could-- or he told us to try to move into them, or try to steal the ball, or you know. So we could kind of be silly doing that too, right?
<v Drew Treybig>我们会站在那儿假装是对方球队的球员，这样他们就可以——或者他告诉我们尝试朝他们移动，或者尝试抢断球，等等。所以我们做这些事时也能显得有点傻，对吧？

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:26.100
<v David Kestenbaum>Was that fun?
<v David Kestenbaum>那好玩吗？

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:33.870
<v Drew Treybig>Yeah. Yeah. It was really fun-- but not on the hot days. I'd say, AJ, I don't want to do this. It's too hot. [LAUGHS]
<v Drew Treybig>是的。是的，真的很有趣——但在天气热的时候就不好了。我会说：“AJ，我不想做了。太热了。”（笑）

00:05:33.870 --> 00:05:45.090
<v David Kestenbaum>Word spreads around town about this great basketball trainer. Other parents hire him. But AJ and Cody, they have something kind of special. They just click, and they have this way of being around each other that seems sweet.
<v David Kestenbaum>关于这位出色的篮球教练的消息在城里传开了。其他家长也雇用了他。但AJ和科迪之间有种特殊的东西。他们一拍即合，相处的方式看起来很甜蜜。

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:59.380
<v David Kestenbaum>When AJ was working with Cody, they would trash talk on the court, which I always thought of as involving a lot of swearing. But AJ taught Cody this really hilarious kind of trash talking-- just messing with someone, asking weird, random questions in the middle of the game. Hey, what did you have for breakfast?
<v David Kestenbaum>当AJ训练科迪时，他们会在球场上说垃圾话，我一直以为那涉及很多脏话。但AJ教了科迪一种非常滑稽的垃圾话——只是戏弄别人，在比赛中问些奇怪、随机的问题。“嘿，你早餐吃了什么？”

00:05:59.380 --> 00:06:18.320
<v Cody Treybig>He would act like my mom sometimes. He would talk in my mom's voice and be like, OK, Cody, make the shot. Time to make the shot. Let's go. Let's get in there and try hard. [LAUGHS] And that would obviously crack me up, and it would be hard to focus.
<v Cody Treybig>他有时会模仿我妈妈。他会用我妈妈的声音说：“好吧，科迪，投篮。是时候投篮了。走吧。进去努力点。”（笑）这显然会让我笑喷，很难集中注意力。

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:32.030
<v David Kestenbaum>Cody played constantly. If the family went on a trip, he would deflate a basketball, stick it in the suitcase, then re-inflate it after they arrived. When you're a kid, you want there to be something that you're special at, or even just a little good at.
<v David Kestenbaum>科迪一直都在打球。如果全家去旅行，他会把篮球放气，塞进手提箱，到达目的地后再重新充气。当你是个孩子时，你希望自己能在某方面特别，或者至少有点擅长。

00:06:32.030 --> 00:06:48.490
<v David Kestenbaum>Because as a kid, you start off being terrible at everything. That is the definition of being a kid. So when you meet someone who helps you get good at something, it can feel amazing. Cody remembers this one afternoon-- maybe fourth grade, on the court out back-- where it all just felt perfect.
<v David Kestenbaum>因为作为一个孩子，你开始时什么都很糟糕。这就是孩子这一词的定义。所以当你遇到一个能帮你擅长某事的人时，那感觉太棒了。科迪记得有一个下午——大概是在四年级，在后院的球场上——感觉一切都很完美。

00:06:48.490 --> 00:07:02.650
<v Cody Treybig>We were just doing jump shots. And I was making like all of them, like never done-- making all of them. And one of my brother's friends had come to hang out with my brother. And he was up at the top watching us. And I noticed that. And I was like-- to me, he was older and cool.
<v Cody Treybig>我们只是在练习跳投。我几乎全都投进了，从来没这样过——全都投进了。我哥哥的一个朋友过来找我哥哥玩。他在上方看着我们。我注意到了。对我来说，他年纪大而且很酷。

00:07:02.650 --> 00:07:17.650
<v Cody Treybig>And I was making them. And he could see I was making them. And I had just this pride, just like it's something I loved, the shooting. And I was doing good at it. And I just remember feeling like I was, yeah, floating on air. It was really nice.
<v Cody Treybig>我投进了。他能看到我投进了。我感到非常自豪，因为这是我热爱的事情，投篮。我做得很好。我记得感觉自己就像，是的，飘在云端。那感觉真好。

00:07:17.650 --> 00:07:18.610
<v David Kestenbaum>And AJ was there?
<v David Kestenbaum>AJ当时也在吗？

00:07:18.610 --> 00:07:22.590
<v Cody Treybig>Yeah. He was coaching me. He was rebounding and giving me the passes.
<v Cody Treybig>是的。他在指导我。他在抢篮板并给我传球。

00:07:22.590 --> 00:07:41.890
<v David Kestenbaum>The next year, Cody got the MVP award for the school team. In some ways, AJ was like a big kid himself. Drew says he often seemed more comfortable with kids than adults. And as Cody gets older, he and AJ really start to become friends. After his games, Cody would always call AJ to tell him how it went.
<v David Kestenbaum>第二年，科迪获得了学校球队的MVP奖。在某些方面，AJ自己就像个大孩子。德鲁说他似乎和孩子相处比和成年人相处更自在。随着科迪长大，他和AJ真的开始成为朋友。比赛后，科迪总会给AJ打电话告诉他表现如何。

00:07:41.890 --> 00:07:53.560
<v Cody Treybig>Which kind of drove my parents nuts, actually, because they wanted to talk to me after the game in the car. And I would be talking to AJ on the phone the whole way home, [CHUCKLES] which just shows how close we were.
<v Cody Treybig>这实际上让我父母抓狂，因为他们想在回家的车上跟我谈谈。而我却全程都在跟AJ打电话（轻笑），这恰恰说明了我们有多亲密。

00:07:53.560 --> 00:08:11.980
<v David Kestenbaum>AJ would sometimes pick Cody up from middle school. Cody started to listen to the music AJ listened to. Young MC, "Bust a Move" was a favorite. When Cody went out clothes shopping with his mom, he picked out clothes that looked like things AJ would wear-- simple, solid color T-shirts. Cody fell in love with basketball. And he kind of fell in love with AJ.
<v David Kestenbaum>AJ有时会从中学接科迪放学。科迪开始听AJ听的音乐。Young MC的《Bust a Move》是他的最爱。当科迪和妈妈去买衣服时，他选的衣服看起来就像AJ会穿的那样——简单的纯色T恤。科迪爱上了篮球。他也某种程度上爱上了AJ。

00:08:11.980 --> 00:08:30.400
<v Cody Treybig>I would hug him and tell him I loved him, yeah. I mean, I would jump up, and he was holding me in the air, or whatever, yeah, and tell him I loved him, yeah. I would get sad when he left. I was shy. But once I got to know people, I got very attached to them.
<v Cody Treybig>我会拥抱他并告诉他我爱他，是的。我的意思是，我会跳起来，他会把我举在空中，或者什么的，是的，告诉他我爱他。他离开时我会感到难过。我很害羞。但一旦我认识了人，我就会变得非常依赖他们。

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:42.240
<v David Kestenbaum>There's something Cody wrote about AJ that sums up how he felt and also weirdly foreshadows what was to come. It's from early on, a school assignment to write about your hero. I had Cody read from it.
<v David Kestenbaum>科迪写过一段关于AJ的话，总结了他的感受，也奇怪地预示了将要发生的事情。这是很早以前学校布置的作业，写关于你的英雄。我让科迪读了一下。

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:50.250
<v Cody Treybig>"Some people in fourth grade might have picked their heroes because they're national celebrities. But I picked mine because he has made me a better person."
<v Cody Treybig>“四年级时，有些人选择他们的英雄是因为他们是国家级名人。但我选择他是因为他让我变成了一个更好的人。”

00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:53.310
<v David Kestenbaum>Cody runs through how they met the way a fourth grader would.
<v David Kestenbaum>科迪按照四年级学生的方式讲述了他们的相遇。

00:08:53.310 --> 00:09:03.990
<v Cody Treybig>"Ding dong goes our doorbell. [CHUCKLES] He was here. I closed my eyes and hoped. Then the door opened. 'Hey, Cody,' he said with happiness. 'Hi, AJ,' I replied."
<v Cody Treybig>“叮咚，门铃响了。（轻笑）他来了。我闭上眼睛祈祷。然后门开了。‘嘿，科迪，’他高兴地说。‘嗨，AJ，’我回答道。”

00:09:03.990 --> 00:09:14.310
<v David Kestenbaum>The essay ends with an interview, Cody asking AJ what seemed like innocuous questions. Where did you grow up? What was your favorite subject in school? And then these two questions.
<v David Kestenbaum>作文结尾是一次采访，科迪问了AJ一些看似无害的问题。你在哪长大的？你在学校最喜欢的科目是什么？然后是这两个问题。

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:32.700
<v Cody Treybig>What values do you think are important? And he says, "Communication and 'Thy shalt not have any gods before me,' and, 'Do unto others as you would have them do unto you.'" Did you have a hero in your life? "Yes, Christ."
<v Cody Treybig>你认为什么价值观很重要？他说：“沟通和‘除了我之外，不可有别神’，以及‘己所欲，施于人’。”你生命中有英雄吗？“是的，基督。”

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:38.490
<v David Kestenbaum>Is it hard to get back in the mind of yourself writing this, when you wrote this?
<v David Kestenbaum>回到写下这些东西时的自己，很难吗？

00:09:38.490 --> 00:09:52.980
<v Cody Treybig>It's very hard, because now I know kind of what was going on. And so it's pretty sad, I think.
<v Cody Treybig>这非常困难，因为现在我知道了当时发生了什么。所以我认为这相当令人悲伤。

00:09:52.980 --> 00:10:01.680
<v David Kestenbaum>Fast forward to the morning of February 7, 2012. Cody's in ninth grade. He and AJ have been hanging out for six years. Here's Drew, Cody's mom.
<v David Kestenbaum>快进到2012年2月7日的早上。科迪上九年级了。他和AJ已经交往了六年。这是德鲁，科迪的妈妈。

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:18.870
<v Drew Treybig>At that point, I was driving Cody to school. And I don't remember why. But I dropped him off. And just as I'm getting ready to leave, my friend whips into the spot next to me. And so I put my window down. She put her window down.
<v Drew Treybig>当时，我正开车送科迪去学校。我不记得为什么了。但我把他放下。就在我准备离开时，我的朋友猛地转进了我旁边的车位。我放下车窗。她也放下车窗。

00:10:18.870 --> 00:10:42.840
<v Drew Treybig>And she's sobbing. She's not even the tearing up kind of person. And she handed me-- she said, oh my god, oh my god. You need to see what AJ has done. I found some transcripts with my son. Here they are.
<v Drew Treybig>她泣不成声。她根本不是那种会流泪的人。她递给我——她说：“哦，天哪，哦，天哪。你需要看看AJ做了什么。我发现了一些我儿子和他的聊天记录。就在这儿。”

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:52.890
<v David Kestenbaum>They were instant messages between the woman's son, Lucas, and AJ. AJ had been coaching Lucas too. And they'd been typing back and forth over Skype. Lucas's mom had printed them out.
<v David Kestenbaum>那是那个女人的儿子卢卡斯（Lucas）和AJ之间的即时通讯记录。AJ也在指导卢卡斯。他们一直在Skype上打字聊天。卢卡斯的妈妈把它们打印了出来。

00:10:52.890 --> 00:11:04.380
<v Drew Treybig>And I read them. And it was as if my entire body went numb. It's still really hard to talk about. [CRYING]
<v Drew Treybig>我读了它们。感觉全身都麻木了。现在谈论这件事仍然非常困难。（哭泣）

00:11:04.380 --> 00:11:18.630
<v David Kestenbaum>There didn't seem to be anything physical going on. The messages were about religion, but not any religion that Drew was familiar with-- stuff from the Bible, but also things that were definitely not in the Bible. Some of it seemed really out there and scary.
<v David Kestenbaum>看起来并没有发生任何身体上的接触。消息是关于宗教的，但不是德鲁熟悉的任何宗教——内容来自圣经，但也包含一些绝对不在圣经里的东西。有些内容看起来非常离谱且吓人。

00:11:18.630 --> 00:11:43.070
<v Drew Treybig>I knew I had to get home. And she told me-- she said, go home right away. You've got to check Cody's Skype transcripts. And I'm an Apple person. I really don't know anything about the PC, the Skyping, and the keyboard even. I mean, everything seems different. So I say to her, I don't know if I'm going to be able to do it.
<v Drew Treybig>我知道我必须回家。她告诉我——她说：“马上回家。你得去查查科迪的Skype记录。”我是个苹果用户。我真的对电脑、Skype和键盘一窍不通。我的意思是，一切看起来都不同。所以我对她说：“我不知道我能不能做到。”

00:11:43.070 --> 00:12:03.810
<v Drew Treybig>I couldn't get home fast enough, because I knew in my heart it was going to be terrible. I got home. Nobody was here. And I went to Cody's room, and his Skype log was up. So he must have been on it that morning right before school.
<v Drew Treybig>我迫不及待地想回家，因为我的内心知道那将是可怕的。我回到家。没人在这儿。我去了科迪的房间，他的Skype记录是打开的。所以他那天早上上学前一定用了它。

00:12:03.810 --> 00:12:17.330
<v David Kestenbaum>She sits there at Cody's desk and starts to read. Cody and AJ had been messaging and Skyping, sometimes late into the night, and clearly trying to hide it from her. The first pages are from some night when she'd been threatening to take his phone away.
<v David Kestenbaum>她坐在科迪的书桌前开始阅读。科迪和AJ一直在互发信息和Skype，有时到深夜，显然是在瞒着她。前几页来自某天晚上，她威胁要没收他的手机时。

00:12:17.330 --> 00:12:36.200
<v David Kestenbaum>Cody writes to AJ, "How do I communicate with you without technology"" AJ, "Please turn off everything and remain calm as possible. This is just for tonight." Cody, "Should I give them my phone if they ask for it?" AJ, "Delete everything and give it to them." She keeps reading, and it gets even stranger.
<v David Kestenbaum>科迪写给AJ：“我如何在没有技术的情况下与你沟通？”AJ：“请关闭所有东西，尽量保持冷静。这只是今晚而已。”科迪：“如果他们问我要，我该把手机给他们吗？”AJ：“删掉所有东西然后给他们。”她继续读着，情况变得更加奇怪了。

00:12:36.200 --> 00:12:49.250
<v Drew Treybig>Rapture, Illuminati, there were references to all these things, biblical or religious, that I was just astounded by.
<v Drew Treybig>被提（Rapture）、光照派（Illuminati），所有这些圣经或宗教的东西，让我感到极其震惊。

00:12:49.250 --> 00:13:04.490
<v David Kestenbaum>It was like Cody and AJ were living in this other world. And it was vast-- some mix of science fiction and stuff inspired by the Bible, particularly the book of Revelation. At the time, Drew couldn't piece it all together. But as best as I can tell, this is the basic outline of it.
<v David Kestenbaum>就像科迪和AJ生活在另一个世界里。那是一个巨大的世界——融合了科幻小说和受圣经启发的素材，特别是《启示录》。当时，德鲁无法把这一切串联起来。但我尽我所能拼凑出了大致的轮廓。

00:13:04.490 --> 00:13:17.370
<v David Kestenbaum>It starts with standard evangelical beliefs about the rapture, the end of days, when a select group of people will suddenly disappear from the Earth to go live with God. And everyone else is going to be left to suffer. Who will be saved, and who will be left behind?
<v David Kestenbaum>它始于关于“被提”的标准福音派信仰，即末日降临时，一小部分人会突然从地球上消失去与上帝同住。其他人则会被留下受苦。谁会被拯救，谁会被抛下？

00:13:17.370 --> 00:13:35.900
<v David Kestenbaum>Well, the devil is trying to capture as many souls as he can. And here's where the sci-fi stuff comes in. The devil is operating through a powerful and secret society called the Illuminati. The Illuminati have built a self-programming supercomputer somewhere in Belgium called "The Beast."
<v David Kestenbaum>好吧，魔鬼正试图捕捉尽可能多的灵魂。这就是科幻元素进入的地方。魔鬼通过一个名为“光照派”的强大而秘密的社会进行运作。光照派在比利时的某个地方建造了一台名为“兽”的自编程超级计算机。

00:13:35.900 --> 00:13:59.520
<v David Kestenbaum>At some point, the Illuminati is going to try to control people by implanting a computer ID chip, an RFID chip, in everyone's hands or foreheads. This chip, it said, is what the Bible has been prophesying, the mark of the beast, a high-tech version of 666. If you get an RFID chip implanted, well, you are definitely not getting saved.
<v David Kestenbaum>在某个时候，光照派将试图通过在每个人的手或前额植入电脑ID芯片，即RFID芯片，来控制人们。这个芯片，上面说，就是圣经所预言的——兽的印记，666的高科技版本。如果你植入RFID芯片，那么，你绝对无法获得拯救。

00:13:59.520 --> 00:14:16.700
<v David Kestenbaum>In the transcripts, AJ and Cody go back and forth about RFID chips a bunch. At one point, AJ writes, "It's here. This coming up year, it will be mandatory for all armed forces, followed by medical field, then everyone else. What it does to the body is inhuman."
<v David Kestenbaum>在聊天记录中，AJ和科迪多次讨论RFID芯片。在某一处，AJ写道：“它来了。明年，所有武装部队都必须植入，接着是医疗领域，然后是其他人。它对身体的作用是不人道的。”

00:14:16.700 --> 00:14:40.430
<v David Kestenbaum>Cody, "What do they do to your body?" AJ, "You lose mind control. Why do you think I took time to study the mind with you?" Cody, "How does it do that?" AJ, "Oh, [BLEEP], here we go. Are you sure? You want to know the truth?" Cody, "Yes, I do." Then the Skype log indicates AJ called him to talk.
<v David Kestenbaum>科迪：“他们对你的身体做了什么？”AJ：“你失去了思想控制。你以为我为什么花时间和你一起研究大脑？”科迪：“它是怎么做到的？”AJ：“哦，该死，开始了。你确定吗？你想知道真相吗？”科迪：“是的，我想知道。”然后Skype记录显示AJ打电话给他详谈。

00:14:40.430 --> 00:14:51.870
<v Drew Treybig>You could see how he was manipulating by reading it. "Yes, there's more. But I guess if you're not ready to hear it--" I mean, you could see him, how he would reel a child in.
<v Drew Treybig>阅读这些内容，你可以看到他是如何操控的。“是的，还有更多。但我猜如果你还没准备好听到的话——”我的意思是，你可以看到他是如何将一个孩子引诱进来的。

00:14:51.870 --> 00:15:06.650
<v David Kestenbaum>AJ writes, "For now, rest. I have so much more to show you. But you have class tomorrow." In some places, the conversation seems very middle school, except they're texting about the apocalypse. And one of them is 36 years old.
<v David Kestenbaum>AJ写道：“现在，休息吧。我还有更多要给你看。但你明天有课。”在某些地方，对话看起来很像初中生的聊天，只是他们在讨论末日。而其中一个人已经36岁了。

00:15:06.650 --> 00:15:19.890
<v David Kestenbaum>Like this time AJ is talking about how he's preparing to assist other followers of the Lord. And Cody compares the whole thing to a movie. Cody, "So it's like you're that guy from The Matrix who rescues Neo and takes him to the refuge underground."
<v David Kestenbaum>比如有次AJ在谈论他如何准备协助主的其他追随者。科迪把整件事比作一部电影。科迪：“所以就像《黑客帝国》里那个救了尼奥并带他去地下避难所的家伙？”

00:15:19.890 --> 00:15:39.390
<v David Kestenbaum>AJ, "You must be talking about Morpheus." Cody, "Yes, that guy." AJ, "How do you know I'm not Neo?" Cody, "But Neo never goes and rescues people from the Matrix." AJ, "But he does show them the truth."
<v David Kestenbaum>AJ：“你一定是在说墨菲斯。”科迪：“是的，那个人。”AJ：“你怎么知道我不是尼奥？”科迪：“但尼奥从来没有去救过人离开矩阵。”AJ：“但他确实向他们展示了真相。”

00:15:39.390 --> 00:15:52.980
<v David Kestenbaum>The most alarming part for Drew reading this is that AJ seems to be trying to slowly separate Cody from his family, to drive a wedge between her and her son. At one point, AJ is telling Cody that his parents are influenced by, quote, "the other side."
<v David Kestenbaum>对德鲁来说，阅读这些内容最令人不安的部分是，AJ似乎试图慢慢地将科迪与他的家人隔离开来，在他和她儿子之间制造隔阂。在某一处，AJ告诉科迪，他的父母受到了来自“另一方”的影响。

00:15:52.980 --> 00:16:00.500
<v Drew Treybig>One of the first things I had read that popped up was him telling Cody to call CPS.
<v Drew Treybig>我读到的第一件事就是他告诉科迪去打举报电话。

00:16:00.500 --> 00:16:01.670
<v David Kestenbaum>Child Protective Services?
<v David Kestenbaum>儿童保护服务局？

00:16:01.670 --> 00:16:06.000
<v Drew Treybig>Yeah, Child Protective-- and to fake that we were abusing him.
<v Drew Treybig>是的，儿童保护服务——并假装我们虐待他。

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:29.780
<v David Kestenbaum>Cody had been asking AJ what he should do if his parents tried to make him get an RFID chip implanted. AJ writes, "CPS, one phone call, 911. You can easily claim abuse, which is not a lie because they would be forcing you into hell. Tell CPS nothing about the chip. Stress potential harm to your body and yelling." Drew reads this and worries that Cody is about to run off with AJ.
<v David Kestenbaum>科迪一直问AJ，如果他父母试图强迫他植入RFID芯片，他该怎么办。AJ写道：“儿童保护服务局，一个电话，911。你可以轻松谎称受到虐待，这不算是撒谎，因为他们是在强迫你进入地狱。什么都不要告诉儿童保护服务局关于芯片的事。强调对你身体的潜在伤害和大吼大叫。”德鲁读到这些，担心科迪快要跟AJ逃跑了。

00:16:29.780 --> 00:16:45.020
<v Drew Treybig>You can just see in this sweet, little, innocent Cody saying things like, well, I think I have some money in a savings account. And then, because AJ is telling him we're going-- he and Cody are going to need a lot of money.
<v Drew Treybig>你可以看到这个甜美、天真、小小的科迪说出类似“嗯，我想我的储蓄账户里有些钱”这样的话。然后，因为AJ告诉他他们要去哪儿——他和科迪将需要很多钱。

00:16:45.020 --> 00:16:47.910
<v David Kestenbaum>Drew calls Jimmy, her husband, who rushes home.
<v David Kestenbaum>德鲁打电话给她的丈夫吉米，他赶回了家。

00:16:47.910 --> 00:16:54.680
<v Jimmy Treybig>I was really afraid. And it was obviously-- it had been going on a very long time.
<v Jimmy Treybig>我真的非常害怕。显然——这已经持续了很长一段时间。

00:16:54.680 --> 00:17:11.150
<v David Kestenbaum>The Skype transcripts only went back a month. But they eventually learned from Cody that he and AJ had been having conversations like this for years-- right there in the house, right under their noses. Drew and Jimmy call the police, but it's unclear if there's anything they can charge AJ with.
<v David Kestenbaum>Skype聊天记录只回溯了一个月。但他们最终从科迪那里得知，他和AJ多年来一直在进行这样的对话——就在家里，就在他们的眼皮底下。德鲁和吉米报了警，但不清楚有什么罪名可以指控AJ。

00:17:11.150 --> 00:17:29.280
<v David Kestenbaum>They decide for sure they need to cut off all contact with AJ, which they worry will upset Cody. They don't know how much of all this religious stuff he really believes. But they know he loves AJ. They need time to figure things out. So they just leave Cody at school for the rest of the day. He's going to classes, doesn't know any of this is happening.
<v David Kestenbaum>他们确定需要切断与AJ的所有联系，他们担心这会令科迪不安。他们不知道科迪到底有多相信这些宗教内容。但他们知道他爱AJ。他们需要时间去弄清楚事情。所以他们那天剩下的时间只是让科迪待在学校。他在上课，根本不知道发生了这一切。

00:17:29.280 --> 00:17:44.520
<v David Kestenbaum>They call a friend who's a lawyer, and they make a plan. When the school day is over, Drew will pick up Cody and also his friend Lucas, who had been messaging with AJ. She'll pretend like everything's normal. But she'll take them directly to the lawyer's office, where Jimmy and the other kid's parents will be.
<v David Kestenbaum>他们打给一位律师朋友，制定了一个计划。放学后，德鲁会去接科迪以及他的朋友卢卡斯，那个一直在跟AJ聊天的人。她会假装一切正常。但她会直接带他们去律师事务所，吉米和其他孩子的家长会等在那里。

00:17:44.520 --> 00:17:53.990
<v Cody Treybig>Last period of the day, last class, I had off period, no class. And I was texting AJ about something I had seen on the internet. And I think he replied. And it was a normal day.
<v Cody Treybig>那天最后一节课，我没课。我正在给AJ发短信关于我在互联网上看到的东西。我想他回复了。那是正常的一天。

00:17:53.990 --> 00:18:09.360
<v Cody Treybig>And my mom-- my friend Lucas and I were hanging out before my mom came and picked me up. And I think we had a basketball session that day with AJ planned. And my mom said, oh, I'm going to drive both of you guys to our house to play.
<v Cody Treybig>我的妈妈——我和卢卡斯在妈妈来接我之前在一起。我想我们那天计划和AJ有一个篮球训练。我妈妈说：“哦，我要开车送你们俩去我家玩。”

00:18:09.360 --> 00:18:26.970
<v Drew Treybig>They were so happy that day when they got in the car. And all I'm thinking is, oh my god, it's just in a couple of minutes when I have to turn left. It's all I kept thinking about. It's just going to all come crashing down. And I don't know if I'm strong enough to do this, because I'm still barely keeping it together.
<v Drew Treybig>他们上车时很高兴。而我心里想的只是，哦，天哪，再过几分钟我就要左转了。这是我脑子里唯一一直在想的事。一切都要崩溃了。我不知道我是否有足够的力量去做这件事，因为我还在勉强支撑着。

00:18:26.970 --> 00:18:41.020
<v Cody Treybig>So that was fine. It was normal. And we were going home. And instead of turning right, we turn left. And Lucas and I were, what's going on? Why are we turning left? And my mom wouldn't answer us. And we could tell something was weird.
<v Cody Treybig>所以那天很好。一切正常。我们在回家路上。本来应该右转的，结果我们向左转了。卢卡斯和我问：“怎么了？为什么我们要左转？”我妈妈没回答我们。我们可以感觉到有什么不对劲。

00:18:41.020 --> 00:18:56.760
<v Cody Treybig>So immediately, Lucas and I are like, what is going on? My mind immediately turned to, oh my god, maybe my parents are taking me somewhere to implant the RFID chip in me. So I'm starting to think about how are we going to get out of this car.
<v Cody Treybig>所以卢卡斯和我立刻就在想，发生什么事了？我的脑子立刻转向，天哪，也许我父母正带我去植入RFID芯片的地方。所以我开始想我们要如何逃离这辆车。

00:18:56.760 --> 00:19:14.040
<v Drew Treybig>The other little boy almost jumped out of the car. And I had to beg him to just please not jump out of the car. They had done nothing wrong. They weren't in trouble. They were going to be OK. But I couldn't tell them. For 20 minutes, that's all I kept saying.
<v Drew Treybig>另一个小男孩差点跳出车外。我不得不求他不要跳车。他们什么都没做错。他们没惹麻烦。他们会没事的。但我不能告诉他们。在那20分钟里，我一直在说那句话。

00:19:14.040 --> 00:19:33.900
<v Cody Treybig>We were texting AJ like, what's going on? Do you know what's going on? We were kind of frantic. She took us to, I guess, our family lawyer at the time's office and sat us down. And they informed us that they had found Lucas's Skype transcripts with AJ.
<v Cody Treybig>我们当时在给AJ发短信说：“怎么了？你知道怎么回事吗？”我们有点慌乱。她把我们带到了我猜是当时我们家律师的办公室，让我们坐下。他们通知我们，他们发现了卢卡斯和AJ的Skype聊天记录。

00:19:33.900 --> 00:19:48.570
<v Cody Treybig>And that prompted my parents to look at my computer. And they found my Skype transcripts with AJ and that they were going to fire him. I wouldn't be allowed to see him anymore and that he wasn't allowed to talk to us anymore.
<v Cody Treybig>这促使我父母查看了我的电脑。他们发现了我和AJ的Skype记录，他们要解雇他。我不被允许再见他，也不被允许再跟他说话。

00:19:48.570 --> 00:19:57.000
<v Cody Treybig>So I mean it-- my world blew up. My world blew up. It was-- I don't know how else to put it. My world just blew up.
<v Cody Treybig>所以我的世界崩溃了。我的世界崩溃了。这——我不知道该怎么形容。我的世界就是崩溃了。

00:19:57.000 --> 00:20:09.420
<v David Kestenbaum>Looking at Cody, though, Drew couldn't tell what was going on with him. He didn't cry. He didn't yell. When they got home, she says she tried to talk to him about all this, but he wouldn't talk. They got him a therapist right away.
<v David Kestenbaum>然而，看着科迪，德鲁无法判断他发生了什么。他没哭。他没喊。当他们到家时，她说她试着跟他谈谈这一切，但他不肯说话。他们立刻给他找了心理医生。

00:20:09.420 --> 00:20:23.430
<v David Kestenbaum>The lawyer told AJ he was fired and to stay away from the Treybigs. The family sent the school a photo of AJ, told them to watch out for him. AJ sent an email to the Treybigs' lawyer, threatening to sue them if they said bad things about him.
<v David Kestenbaum>律师告诉AJ他被解雇了，并且离特雷比格一家远点。这家人发给学校一张AJ的照片，提醒他们提防他。AJ给特雷比格一家的律师发了一封电子邮件，威胁说如果他们说他坏话，就要起诉他们。

00:20:23.430 --> 00:20:32.380
<v David Kestenbaum>Cody had been a high honors student. But the next day, he got a 24 on a test. After school, he'd go to his room, stay in there, and go to sleep.
<v David Kestenbaum>科迪曾经是优等生。但第二天，他在考试中只得了24分。放学后，他会回到房间，待在里面，然后睡觉。

00:20:32.380 --> 00:20:52.130
<v David Kestenbaum>In the mornings, his parents would break down. They'd say, please just come home to us today. Don't run away with him. Of course, one of the weird things about this whole situation is that before this, Cody had seemed totally normal around the house, just his regular self.
<v David Kestenbaum>早上，他的父母会崩溃。他们会说：“求求你，今天回到我们身边吧。不要和他一起逃跑。”当然，整件事奇怪的地方在于，在此之前，科迪在家里看起来完全正常，就是平常的样子。

00:20:52.130 --> 00:21:14.630
<v David Kestenbaum>When I first heard this story, I honestly didn't know what to think. It seemed incredible that all this could be going on and that no one would notice. How did this happen? And why did AJ do it? The Treybigs thought he was grooming Cody for years in some evil plan to control him and pull him away from the family. But maybe AJ was just a devoted religious guy with unconventional beliefs.
<v David Kestenbaum>当我第一次听到这个故事时，老实说我不知道该想什么。难以置信这一切竟在发生，而没人注意到。这是怎么发生的？AJ为什么要这么做？特雷比格一家认为他多年来一直在培养科迪，怀揣着某种邪恶计划来控制他，把他从家里拉走。但也许AJ只是一个有着非传统信仰的忠诚宗教人士。

00:21:14.630 --> 00:21:26.240
