<v David Kestenbaum>AJ会在学校期间打手机给他们，说类似“你在安全位置吗？”之类的话。这很可怕，但也挺让人兴奋，就像你是特工一样。但科迪是AJ花时间最多的人，他相信了一切。

00:31:11.340 --> 00:31:25.570
<v David Kestenbaum>AJ would call him on his cell phone during school and say something like, are you in a secure location? It was scary, but also kind of exciting, like you were a secret agent. But Cody was the one AJ spent the most time with, and he believed it all.
<v David Kestenbaum>他的朋友告诉我他们根本不知道。在他脑子里，就像科迪生活在电影里。事实证明，它确实是一部1972年的电影，名叫《被提》。

00:31:25.570 --> 00:31:37.970
<v David Kestenbaum>His friends told me they had no idea. In his head, it was like Cody was living inside a movie. And as it turns out, it is an actual movie from 1972 called The Rapture.
<v David Kestenbaum>整个关于比利时有一台名为“兽”的超级计算机，连接到人们被标记数字的故事——就是出自那里。我没能得到那部电影的副本。但我们找到了一张LP唱片，里面有一个《世界大战》风格的广播剧版本。

00:31:37.970 --> 00:31:54.530
<v David Kestenbaum>That's where this whole story about a supercomputer called "The Beast," in Belgium, connected to people being marked with numbers-- that's where it comes from. I wasn't able to get a copy of the film. But we were able to find an LP which has a radio drama version of it in the style of War of the Worlds.
<v David Kestenbaum>那台电脑将为世界的每个公民分配一个数字，用于所有购买和出售。我们正在设置，让每个人都被纹上一个隐形标记。我已经把我的数字纹在手上和前额上了。

00:31:54.530 --> 00:32:12.560
<v Man 1>The computer will assign each citizen of the world a number for use for all buying and all selling. We're setting it up so that every person will be tattooed with an invisible mark. I've already had my number tattooed on my hand and forehead.
<v Man 1>那太神奇了。

00:32:12.560 --> 00:32:13.880
<v Man 2>That's amazing.
<v Man 2>我追踪到了编剧，一位名叫乔·穆瑟（Joe Musser）的基督徒作家。他说电影的一些宣传材料是虚构的新闻报道形式，有些人以为是真的。它们在教堂里流传，甚至在一本基督教杂志上发表过。

00:32:13.880 --> 00:32:27.800
<v David Kestenbaum>I tracked down the screenwriter, a Christian author named Joe Musser. He said some of the promotional material for the movie was in the form of fictional news stories, which some people thought were real. They got passed around in churches and even published in one Christian magazine.
<v David Kestenbaum>他写信说：“嘿，你们搞砸了。这不是真的。”但已经太晚了。40多年后，比利时有一台名为“兽”的超级计算机的想法，仍然存在。

00:32:27.800 --> 00:32:38.670
<v David Kestenbaum>He wrote in to say, hey, you messed up. This isn't true. But it was too late. 40-something years later, the idea of a supercomputer in Belgium called "The Beast," it's still around.
<v David Kestenbaum>科迪说他早上醒来会看CNN，看看被提是否发生了，因为毫无疑问，它会出现在新闻里。AJ说的事情很可怕，但唯一安全的地方是AJ身边。所以他没有逃跑，而是回到他身边。

00:32:38.670 --> 00:32:53.510
<v David Kestenbaum>Cody says he would wake up in the morning and check CNN to see if the rapture had happened, because for sure, it would be on the news. The things AJ said were terrifying, but the only safe place was with AJ. So instead of running away, he'd go back to him.
<v David Kestenbaum>我自己的思想也不安全。我有一种抽动，因为我太害怕在脑子里说“我恨上帝”，以至于我必须一直在脑子里说话。我必须说出“我非常爱上帝。上帝太神奇了。我太爱他了”这样的短语。就像我——我爱上帝。我爱上帝。

00:32:53.510 --> 00:33:14.390
<v Cody Treybig>My own thoughts weren't safe. I had this tic where I was so afraid that I would say "I hate God" in my head that if I-- I had to talk constantly in my head. I had to say the phrase, I love God so much. God is so amazing. I love him so much. Like I'm-- I love God. I love God.
<v Cody Treybig>那将是我每一天醒着的每一个小时。我必须在脑子里不断地说出这句话，否则我担心我会把“我恨上帝”这句话说出来。然后我就会——我就会永远下地狱。我连我自己都不安全。

00:33:14.390 --> 00:33:34.980
<v Cody Treybig>And that would be every waking hour of my day. I would have to say that constantly throughout in my head, or else I was afraid I would let the phrase "I hate God" out. And then I would be-- I would go to hell forever. I wasn't even safe with my own self.
<v Cody Treybig>科迪把这一切瞒着父母三年。就像在篮球中，你有暗号让队友知道你在跑什么战术一样，他和AJ也有一个。

00:33:34.980 --> 00:33:44.700
<v David Kestenbaum>Cody hid all this from his parents for three years. And just like in basketball, how you had codes so your teammates knew what play you were running, he and AJ had one too.
<v David Kestenbaum>他甚至可能在我父母面前说什么：“嘿，我前几天和我的朋友约翰说话。他告诉我在一场比赛里他得了12分，抢了4个篮板。”我知道那意味着“去读圣经，约翰福音第12章，第4节”。

00:33:44.700 --> 00:34:01.530
<v Cody Treybig>He might say, even in front of my parents or something, hey, I was talking to my friend John the other day. And he told me he had 12 points and 4 rebounds in a game. And I knew that meant go read in the Bible, John, chapter 12, or whatever, verse 4.
<v Cody Treybig>科迪的妈妈说她确实注意到科迪对宗教更感兴趣了。他会缠着她让他跟AJ谈论宗教。她说不。在某个时候，科迪要求接受洗礼。他妈妈担心那可能意味着AJ还在跟科迪谈论宗教，而他承诺过不再谈的。

00:34:01.530 --> 00:34:17.860
<v David Kestenbaum>Cody's mom says she did notice Cody was more interested in religion. He would pester her to let him talk to AJ about religion. She said no. At some point, Cody asked to get baptized. His mom worried that might mean that AJ was still talking to Cody about religion, which he'd promised not to do.
<v David Kestenbaum>但科迪坚持说：“不，不，这只是我想做的事。”所以他们答应了。德鲁说，回想起来，这一切看起来当然非常不同。

00:34:17.860 --> 00:34:26.950
<v David Kestenbaum>But Cody insisted, no, no, this is just something I want to do. So they said OK. Drew says, in retrospect, of course, all this looks very different.
<v David Kestenbaum>我觉得，作为一个妈妈，我怎么会不知道呢？这个小男孩以为他每天、每一分钟都要死去。我的意思是，我怎么可能不知道呢？

00:34:26.950 --> 00:34:43.030
<v Drew Treybig>I feel like, as a mom, how could I not know? This little boy thought he was going to die every day, every minute of every day. I mean, how could I have not known that?
<v Drew Treybig>我的意思是，就像科迪一直生活在这个不是真实世界的世界里。但你也生活在一个不是真实世界的世界里，因为这一切正在发生，而你却不知道。

00:34:43.030 --> 00:34:55.480
<v David Kestenbaum>I mean, it's like Cody's been living in this world that is not the real world. But you were also living in this world that was not the real world because this was going on, and you didn't know it.
<v David Kestenbaum>对。对。专家、精神病学家等等，他们会告诉你，你必须原谅自己。我想我就是做不到。我想我的重心仍然在科迪身上。

00:34:55.480 --> 00:35:18.030
<v Drew Treybig>Right. Right. The specialists and the psychiatrists and all, they'll tell you that you have to forgive yourself. I guess I just don't. I think my focus is just still on Cody.
<v Drew Treybig>我向一些关于邪教和洗脑的专家咨询过。当我告诉他们这个故事时，他们的反应就像医生看到有人带着他们认为的一组不寻常症状出现，但医生其实已经见过一百次一样。

00:35:18.030 --> 00:35:30.300
<v David Kestenbaum>I talked to some experts about cults and brainwashing. And when I told them this story, they reacted the way doctors do when someone comes in with what they think is an unusual set of symptoms but that the doctor's seen a hundred times.
<v David Kestenbaum>没有邪教的官方定义。但他们都说：“是的，听起来像是一个。”邪教往往承诺了解整个世界的关键。追随者被迫保守所有秘密。整个事情逐渐建立，以至于很难在不承认自己一直错误的情况下退出。

00:35:30.300 --> 00:35:46.650
<v David Kestenbaum>There's no official definition of a cult. But they all said, yeah, that sounds like one. Cults often promise the key to understanding the whole world. Followers are pressured to keep everything secret. The whole thing gets built up gradually, so it becomes hard to back out without admitting you were wrong the whole time.
<v David Kestenbaum>这一切的顶端是一个有魅力的领袖。我问邪教领袖是否知道他们是邪教领袖。答案是，有时知道。这些专家告诉我，没有精神疾病史的完全健康的人也会掉进邪教里。

00:35:46.650 --> 00:36:00.950
<v David Kestenbaum>At the top of it all is a charismatic leader. I asked if cult leaders knew they were cult leaders. The answer, sometimes. These experts told me perfectly healthy people with no history of mental illness fall into cults.
<v David Kestenbaum>整个事情能运作的部分原因是领袖引入了一些值得恐惧的东西，同时提供解决恐惧的方案，这创造了一种非常紧密的联系。这种技巧当然在很多地方都被使用——广告、政治竞选。在这里只是调到了最大程度。

00:36:00.950 --> 00:36:19.120
<v David Kestenbaum>Part of what makes the whole thing work is that the leader introduces something to be afraid of, while also providing the solution to that fear, which creates this really strong bond. That technique, of course, is used in lots of places-- advertising, political campaigns. Here it's just all turned up to 10.
<v David Kestenbaum>科迪的爸爸告诉我，这段经历让他理解了新闻中关于父母有一天醒来发现其中一个孩子消失并去加入ISIS的故事。专家告诉我这个案例中感觉不寻常的一点是，这似乎是他们所说的“一对一邪教”——一个领袖，一个追随者。这些可能特别难化解。在非常真实的意义上，就像有人在科迪的大脑里植入了芯片。

00:36:19.120 --> 00:36:56.580
<v David Kestenbaum>Cody's dad told me that this experience helped him understand those stories in the news about parents who wake up one day to discover one of their kids has disappeared and gone off to join ISIS. The one thing the experts told me felt unusual about this case to them is that it seemed to be what they called a one-on-one cult-- one leader, one follower. Those can be particularly hard to undo. In a very real way, it was as if someone had implanted a chip in Cody's brain.
<v David Kestenbaum>大卫·凯斯滕鲍姆。接下来，科迪的父母试图把芯片从科迪的大脑里取出来，正如你所料，这非常困难。一分钟后，来自芝加哥公共广播电台，我们的节目继续。

00:36:56.580 --> 00:37:11.730
<v Ira Glass>David Kestenbaum. Coming up, Cody's parents try to get the chip out of Cody's brain, which, as you might expect, is really hard. That's in a minute, from Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天的节目是《我大脑里的芯片》。在今天节目的上半部分，如果你刚调到这个频道，我们听到了科迪·特雷比格是如何从他的篮球教练那里得到各种科幻宗教信仰的。在节目的下半部分，他的父母试图让他不再相信那些东西。再次，这是大卫·凯斯滕鲍姆。

00:37:11.730 --> 00:37:31.320
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today's program, Chip in My Brain. In the first half of today's show, if you're just tuning in, we heard how Cody Treybig got all kinds of sci-fi religious beliefs from his basketball coach. In this half of the show, his parents try to get him to unbelieve that stuff. Again, here's David Kestenbaum.
<v Ira Glass>科迪说在最初的治疗中，实际上在第一年的大部分时间里，他有一部分是超级愤怒的。比如，“为什么我非要去看心理医生？我没问题。问题在于你们。”

00:37:31.320 --> 00:37:43.040
<v David Kestenbaum>Cody says in the initial therapy sessions, actually for most of the first year, part of him was super angry. Like, why do I have to go to a therapist? There's nothing wrong with me. The problem is with you.
<v David Kestenbaum>他会有这些小脾气。他的妈妈德鲁会看着他躲在房间里几个小时，她只能等着。有时她会说：“求求你，求求你，跟我说说。”但科迪会爆发。“为什么我不能跟AJ在一起？我们不能都跟他坐下来解决这个问题吗？”

00:37:43.040 --> 00:37:58.250
<v David Kestenbaum>He'd have these tantrums. His mom, Drew, would watch him hole up in his room for hours, and she'd just have to wait. Sometimes she'd say, please, please, talk to me. But Cody would just explode. Why can't I be with AJ? Can't we all just sit down with him and work this out?
<v David Kestenbaum>情况没有好转，反而出现了这些他陷入那个世界有多深的提醒。比如有一天完全让德鲁措手不及。他们正准备去一个很久以前就计划好的特别家庭旅行。

00:37:58.250 --> 00:38:09.510
<v David Kestenbaum>Instead of getting better, there were just these reminders of how deep in this world he was. Like this one day took Drew totally by surprise. They were preparing to go on a special family trip they had planned ages ago.
<v David Kestenbaum>他们要去伦敦参加奥运会，记得吗，那正是AJ说可能标志着末日降临、被提可能发生的活动，这意味着光照派将试图给每个人植入RFID芯片。德鲁当时对此一无所知。但他们准备出发的那个早上，科迪就是不肯下床。

00:38:09.510 --> 00:38:29.270
<v David Kestenbaum>They were going to go to London for the Olympics which, remember, was the very event AJ had said might mark the end of days, when the rapture could happen, which meant the Illuminati were going to be trying to implant everybody with RFID chips. Drew didn't know any of that at the time. But the morning they were getting ready to go, Cody would not get out of bed.
<v David Kestenbaum>所以我——我躺在他身边。我搂住他。我说：“科迪，我们真的需要去机场了。”

00:38:29.270 --> 00:38:41.510
<v Drew Treybig>So I got-- I laid down next to him. And I put my arms around him. And I said, Cody, we really need to leave for the airport.
<v Drew Treybig>我说：“快把衣服穿上吧。你的行李都打包好了。我们走吧。”他用最虚弱的声音告诉我他不能去，就像他已经放弃了一样。他说：“妈妈，我不能，因为我要被杀掉了。”

00:38:41.510 --> 00:38:59.570
<v Drew Treybig>I said, let's just throw some clothes on you. Your suitcase is packed. Let's just go. And he told me he couldn't in just the weakest voice, almost like he'd given up. He said, Mom, I can't because I'm going to get killed.
<v Drew Treybig>德鲁和她丈夫吉米告诉我，他们都觉得，好吧，这是一个我们可以展示给他看，向他证明这一切都不是真实的时刻。只要去奥运会就好了。你会看到的。

00:38:59.570 --> 00:39:09.950
<v David Kestenbaum>Drew and her husband Jimmy told me they both felt like, OK, here is a moment where we can show him, prove to him, that none of this is real. Just come to the Olympics. You'll see.
<v David Kestenbaum>但他不肯去。他做不到。

00:39:09.950 --> 00:39:12.680
<v Drew Treybig>But he wouldn't go. He couldn't do it.
<v Drew Treybig>科迪认为如果我们去那里就会死掉，这事实很难理解。所以我的意思是，无论逻辑是什么，在精神上，我们怎么谈论都不重要。所以事后，你会进行关于许多不同事情的谈话，你无法真正理解。

00:39:12.680 --> 00:39:37.940
<v Jimmy Treybig>The fact that Cody thought we were going to die if we went there, that was a hard thing to understand. So I mean, no matter what the logic was, mentally, there was something that it didn't matter how we talked. So afterwards, you would have conversations like that about many different things that you couldn't really understand.
<v Jimmy Treybig>你如何让某人相信他们的整个世界观是错的？科迪心理医生的看法是你做不到。科迪将不得不自己决定。也许如果你能消除恐惧和焦虑，它会使他的精神免疫系统（如果找不到更好的词的话）发挥作用。科迪将开始自己审视比利时有超级计算机和光照派的想法，并自己想通这一切。

00:39:37.940 --> 00:40:02.430
<v David Kestenbaum>How do you convince someone that their whole worldview is wrong? Cody's therapist's take was that you couldn't. Cody was going to have to decide for himself. Maybe if you could take away the fear and the anxiety, it would allow his mental immune system, for lack of a better word, to function. Cody would start to examine the idea of a supercomputer in Belgium and the Illuminati and think it all through for himself.
<v David Kestenbaum>那时，很多事情都在科迪身上引发了一种恐怖。如果他被什么东西绊倒或错失罚球，嗯，AJ告诉过他那是魔鬼就在附近的迹象。心理医生给了科迪一些在感到恐慌时让自己冷静下来的方法——呼吸练习，专注于简单的事实。“我在爱我的人身边。”

00:40:02.430 --> 00:40:21.480
<v David Kestenbaum>Back then, lots of things were triggering a kind of terror in Cody. If he'd trip on something or miss foul shots, well, AJ had told him that was a sign the devil was close by. The therapist gave Cody ways to calm himself down when he felt that panic coming-- breathing exercises, simple facts to focus on. I'm around people who love me.
<v David Kestenbaum>一位处理过此类案件的咨询师以一种真正触动我的方式表达了这一点。他说，你必须帮助苍蝇找到出路离开瓶子，理念是瓶子里的苍蝇不知道它在瓶子里，因为墙壁是透明的。但如果你能让它看到瓶子，它就能找到出路。瓶子里的苍蝇是维特根斯坦写的东西的参考，他是在谈论哲学家。但无论如何，我觉得它很有用。

00:40:21.480 --> 00:40:50.060
<v David Kestenbaum>One counselor who has handled cases like this put it in this way that really stuck with me. He said, you have to help the fly find its way out of the bottle, the idea being that a fly in a bottle doesn't know it's in a bottle because the walls are transparent. But if you can get it to see the bottle, it can find its way out. The fly in a bottle is a reference to something Wittgenstein wrote, and he was talking about philosophers. But whatever, I found it useful.
<v David Kestenbaum>科迪的心理医生从未告诉科迪他相信的是错的。他只是提问，尝试创造一个科迪感到安全的地方，谈论这些事情并思考它们。科迪确实开始质疑事物。

00:40:50.060 --> 00:41:01.980
<v David Kestenbaum>Cody's therapist never told Cody that what he believed was wrong. He just asked questions, tried to create a place where Cody felt safe to talk about these things and to think about them. Cody did start to question things.
<v David Kestenbaum>他没有去阅读关于RFID芯片和光照派的内容，而是开始在网上阅读无神论者和宗教学者之间的辩论。他开始喜爱理查德·道金斯（Richard Dawkins）和克里斯托弗·希钦斯（Christopher Hitchens）。但不相信某事很难。

00:41:01.980 --> 00:41:14.700
<v David Kestenbaum>Instead of reading about RFID chips and the Illuminati, Cody started reading debates between atheists and religious scholars online. He grew to love Richard Dawkins and Christopher Hitchens. But unbelieving something is hard.
<v David Kestenbaum>你不想放弃那个你参与其中的特殊事物。对我来说，回到正常生活并认识到这一切仅此而已——这只是生活，你创造自己的意义——意识到并没有我参与其中的那个特殊事物，这一点真的很难。那只是编出来的。

00:41:14.700 --> 00:41:48.060
<v Cody Treybig>You don't want to give up that special thing that you're a part of. It was really hard for me to get back to normalcy in life and recognizing that this is all there is to it-- it's just life, and you make your own meaning-- to get to the point where I realized that there wasn't this special thing that I was a part of. It was just made up.
<v Cody Treybig>随着时间推移，似乎奏效了。科迪开始有了一些感觉平静的时刻，那些正常的小岛，他不会有疯狂的想法。科迪记得有次治疗后开车回家的情景。

00:41:48.060 --> 00:41:59.400
<v David Kestenbaum>Over time, it seemed to be working. Cody started having these moments where he would feel at peace, little islands of normalcy where he wouldn't have crazy thoughts. Cody remembers driving home after this one therapy session.
<v David Kestenbaum>在那次车程里，我很开心。我很开心。我只希望它永远持续下去。我想也许会。当然，一旦你开始想，“哦，我很开心”，然后“我希望它不会消失”。然后你就开始吓唬自己。然后一切就结束了。

00:41:59.400 --> 00:42:24.090
<v Cody Treybig>For that one car ride, I was happy. I was happy. And I just wanted it to last forever. And I thought maybe it would. And of course, once you start to think, oh, I'm happy, and then I hope it doesn't go away. And then you start to scare yourself. And then it's over.
<v Cody Treybig>科迪说他的高二那年，仍然深陷AJ描述的那个世界。高三和高四，他可能是一半进去，一半出来。比如如果你问他是否相信光照派，他会说不。但就像他内心深处仍然相信一样。他不断地有强烈的恐慌和焦虑发作。

00:42:24.090 --> 00:42:42.370
<v David Kestenbaum>Cody says his sophomore year, he was still deep in the world that AJ had described. Junior and senior year, he was probably half in, half out. Like if you asked him if he believed in the Illuminati, he would say no. But it was like some deep part of him still did. He kept having these intense episodes of panic and anxiety.
<v David Kestenbaum>科迪的一个朋友告诉我，科迪不会谈论他受到多么深的影响，以及这些东西仍然伴随着他。但他们能看出他过得很艰难。一个朋友说：“我们试着帮他。但我们懂什么？我们才18岁。”

00:42:42.370 --> 00:43:00.800
<v David Kestenbaum>One of Cody's friends told me that Cody wouldn't talk about how deeply he'd been affected and how this stuff was still with him. But they could tell he was having a hard time. We tried to help him, one said. But what did we know? We were 18 years old.
<v David Kestenbaum>科迪不得不从高中休学。然后他回去了，完成了学业。他申请了大学，被录取了。科迪想，如果我能做到这一点，如果我能去大学并度过那个阶段，那就像是一份理智证明，一个我会好起来的标志。

00:43:00.800 --> 00:43:14.860
<v David Kestenbaum>Cody had to take a medical leave from high school. Then he went back, finished. He applied to college, got in. And Cody thought, if I can do this, if I can go to college and get through that, it'll be like a certificate of sanity, a sign that I'm going to be OK.
<v David Kestenbaum>离家只有两个小时。他妈妈告诉他，随时打电话。我能跳上车，两小时内就会赶到。事情看起来顺利了一段时间。但她知道他在挣扎。然后她接到了电话。

00:43:14.860 --> 00:43:27.600
<v David Kestenbaum>It was just two hours from home. His mom told him, call anytime. I can get in a car, and I'll be there in just two hours. Things seemed to be going OK for a while. But she knew he was struggling. And then she got a call.
<v David Kestenbaum>“我做不到了，”他说，“我做不到了。”她开车去接他，去了他的宿舍，那时她看到了他是怎么生活的。科迪曾向她提到他垫高了床，她以为他把它改造成了阁楼之类的地方来腾出更多空间。但那不是。他把它垫高了一点点，并睡在下面那个小空间里。

00:43:27.600 --> 00:43:49.460
<v David Kestenbaum>I can't do it anymore, he said. I can't do it. She drove to get him, went to his dorm room, which is when she saw how he'd been living. Cody had mentioned to her that he had raised his bed, which she thought meant that he'd converted it into a loft or something to make more space. But that wasn't it. He'd raised it a tiny bit and was sleeping under it in this little space.
<v David Kestenbaum>当我走进那儿，看到我儿子已经这样睡了几个月又几个月又几个月——那儿可能只有几英寸——我的意思是，我甚至不知道他是怎么滑进去的。从地面到床底的空间太小了。

00:43:49.460 --> 00:44:11.690
<v Drew Treybig>When I walked in there, and I saw how my son had been sleeping for months and months and months-- there were maybe inches from-- I mean I don't even know how he could have slid under there. The space was so small from the ground to the bottom of the bed.
<v Drew Treybig>我意识到那是一个藏身之处。他不是在睡觉。他在藏身。我的意思是，那是他的——我的意思是，他实际上是在藏身。那就是他生活的地方。

00:44:11.690 --> 00:44:25.510
<v Drew Treybig>And I realized that was a hiding spot. He wasn't sleeping. He was hiding. Like that was his version of a-- I mean, he was literally hiding. And that's where he was living.
<v Drew Treybig>我记得当我看到那个时想——我的意思是，我说：“科迪，这就是你一直睡的地方？”他说：“是啊，妈妈。”我不希望他看到我难过，所以我走出了他的房间。我崩溃了，因为我想，我们要——他撑不过去了。我们会失去他。我不是说比喻意义上。我们会真地失去这个孩子。我们会失去他。

00:44:25.510 --> 00:44:52.520
<v Drew Treybig>And I remember thinking when I saw that-- I mean, I said, Cody, this is where you've been sleeping? And he said, yeah, Mom. And I didn't want him to see me get upset, so I walked out of his room. And I broke down because I thought, we're going to-- he's not going to make it. We're going to lose him. And I don't mean figuratively. We are going to literally lose this kid. We are going to lose him.
<v Drew Treybig>科迪后来曾说他考虑过自杀。这似乎是让这一切停止的唯一方法。科迪和妈妈一起开车回家。到此时已经是四年了——四年的，正如科迪所说，尝试修理他自己的大脑。

00:44:52.520 --> 00:45:14.280
<v David Kestenbaum>Cody later said he had thought about killing himself. It seemed like it might be the only way to make this stop. Cody and his mom drove home together. It had been four years at this point-- four years of, as Cody puts it, trying to fix his own brain.
<v David Kestenbaum>在科迪搬回家后不久，他的父母告诉他有些事他们需要跟他谈谈。他们相当确定这是个坏主意。但他们觉得他们必须告诉他，因为如果他想做，他需要尽快决定。

00:45:14.280 --> 00:45:26.520
<v David Kestenbaum>Sometime after Cody moves home, his parents tell him there's something they need to talk to him about. They're pretty sure it's a bad idea. But they feel like they have to tell him, because if he wants to do it, he needs to decide soon.
<v David Kestenbaum>这涉及法院，不是篮球场。他们告诉科迪如果他愿意，他可以尝试起诉AJ。这很难。法律没有很好的方式来处理这类案件。

00:45:26.520 --> 00:45:39.350
<v David Kestenbaum>It involved the court, and not a basketball one. They told Cody if he wanted to, he could try to sue AJ. It was a long shot. The law doesn't have a good way to deal with cases like this.
<v David Kestenbaum>毕竟，没人被绑架。没钱被偷。科迪说没发生过任何身体接触。他们所做的只是谈话。事实上，是谈论宗教，这在我国是受到明确保护的。它写在宪法里。

00:45:39.350 --> 00:45:53.890
<v David Kestenbaum>After all, no one was kidnapped. No money was stolen. Cody says nothing physical ever happened. All they'd done was talk. and in fact, talk about religion, which is specifically protected in this country. It's in the Constitution.
<v David Kestenbaum>科迪的父母做了一些调查。最好的途径似乎是起诉“蓄意造成精神痛苦”。他们必须证明的法律描述是令人生畏的。这种行为必须是——引用——“性质极其恶劣，程度极其极端，超出了所有体面的界限，在文明社会中被视为残暴且完全不可容忍的。”

00:45:53.890 --> 00:46:17.560
<v David Kestenbaum>Cody's parents had done some research. And the best route seemed to be to sue for intentional infliction of emotional distress. The legal description of what they would have to show was daunting. The conduct had to be, quote, "So outrageous in character and so extreme in degree as to go beyond all possible bounds of decency, and to be regarded as atrocious and utterly intolerable in a civilized community."
<v David Kestenbaum>如果科迪选择这么做，整个事情可能需要几年。科迪将不得不与AJ待在法庭里。他将不得不公开详细地重温整个事情，同时对方律师会试图阻挠他。最终，他可能会输。或者换句话说，AJ可能会赢。而思考的时间不多了。

00:46:17.560 --> 00:46:34.910
<v David Kestenbaum>If Cody chose to do it, the whole thing could take years. Cody would have to be in a courtroom with AJ. He would have to relive the whole thing in detail in public, while an opposing attorney tried to derail him. In the end, he might lose. Or put another way, AJ might win. And there wasn't much time to think about it.
<v David Kestenbaum>科迪的生日就在几周内。诉讼时效就要到了。所以他需要在生日前的两到两周半内决定。

00:46:34.910 --> 00:46:52.910
<v Drew Treybig>Cody's birthday was coming in just a couple of weeks. And the statute of limitations was going to run out. So he would need to decide within the next two, two and a half weeks before his birthday.
<v Drew Treybig>科迪考虑了一下。在他生日前一天，他提交了起诉的文件。经过了一年多的法律纠缠。但去年九月，它进入了审判阶段。我当时在场，全程参与了开始部分。

00:46:52.910 --> 00:47:11.780
<v David Kestenbaum>Cody thought about it. And one day before his birthday, he filed the paperwork to go ahead. There was over a year of legal wrangling. But this past September, it went to trial. I was there for the beginning of it.
<v David Kestenbaum>科迪在审判前有很多担忧。如果他编造了这一切怎么办？他说那其实出现在他脑海里。或者如果当他把这一切告诉陪审团时，他们认为他自己有什么问题，而不是AJ怎么办？

00:47:11.780 --> 00:47:24.170
<v David Kestenbaum>Cody had all kinds of worries leading up to it. What if he had made all this up? He said that actually crossed his mind. Or what if, when he told all this to the jury, they thought there was something wrong with him instead of AJ?
<v David Kestenbaum>科迪在审判前的几天里有恐慌发作。就像他倒退了。但他站在了陪审团面前。

00:47:24.170 --> 00:47:32.730
<v David Kestenbaum>Cody had panic attacks in the days before the trial. It was like he'd slid backward. But he took the stand in front of the jury.
<v David Kestenbaum>第一天，我记得非常清楚。我们在健身房里。然后——

00:47:32.730 --> 00:47:49.700
<v Cody Treybig>The first day, I can remember so vividly. We were in my weight room. And--
<v Cody Treybig>科迪的律师说这是一个——引用——“对一个小男孩的秘密操纵和隔离”案件。他称发生的事情是心理折磨。我看陪审团，但他们无法被读懂。他们坐在那儿完全面无表情。科迪在证人席上待了超过七个小时。但他的部分结束了。

00:47:49.700 --> 00:48:07.760
<v David Kestenbaum>Cody's lawyer said this was a case of, quote, "The secret manipulation and isolation of a young boy." He called what happened psychological torture. I looked at the jury, but they were impossible to read. They sat there totally expressionless. Cody was on the stand for over seven hours. But then his part was done.
<v David Kestenbaum>先生，请为了记录报出你的名字。

00:48:07.760 --> 00:48:11.440
<v Clerk>Would you state your name for the record, sir?
<v Clerk>亚瑟·劳伦斯二世（Arthur Lawrence, Jr.）。

00:48:11.440 --> 00:48:14.780
<v Aj Lawrence>Arthur Lawrence, Jr.
<v Aj Lawrence>你叫AJ吗？

00:48:14.780 --> 00:48:16.720
<v Clerk>And do you go by AJ?
<v Clerk>是的。

00:48:16.720 --> 00:48:17.980
<v Aj Lawrence>Yes.
<v Aj Lawrence>这是AJ终于出现了。他衣着整洁，带着一本圣经，是的，个子很高，几乎挤不进证人席。

00:48:17.980 --> 00:48:25.540
<v David Kestenbaum>And here, finally, is AJ. He was neatly dressed, carrying a Bible, and yes, tall, barely fit in the witness stand.
<v David Kestenbaum>我的下背部太难受了。它在收紧，我对此表示歉意。

00:48:25.540 --> 00:48:30.160
<v Aj Lawrence>My lower back is killing me. It's tightening up, and I apologize for that.
<v Aj Lawrence>如果你需要更多空间，我们可以移走那两本笔记本。那个小桌子可以折叠起来，如果有帮助的话。

00:48:30.160 --> 00:48:37.160
<v Woman>If you need more space, we can move those two notebooks. And that little table will fold down, if that's helpful.
<v Woman>嗯，我指的是这整个木质东西。

00:48:37.160 --> 00:48:39.160
<v Aj Lawrence>Well, I meant this entire wooden thing.
<v Aj Lawrence>OK，OK，我不能移走所有那些。

00:48:39.160 --> 00:48:40.490
<v Aj Lawrence>OK, OK, I can't remove all of that.
<v Aj Lawrence>（笑声）

00:48:40.490 --> 00:48:42.700
<v Aj Lawrence>[LAUGHTER]
<v Aj Lawrence>先生，你多高？

00:48:42.700 --> 00:48:44.080
<v Attorney 1>How tall are you, sir?
<v Attorney 1>6英尺6英寸。

00:48:44.080 --> 00:48:45.140
<v Aj Lawrence>6' 6".
<v Aj Lawrence>好的。劳伦斯先生，你曾经被判过重罪吗？

00:48:45.140 --> 00:48:50.680
<v Attorney 1>OK. Mr. Lawrence, have you ever been convicted of a felony?
<v Attorney 1>没有。

00:48:50.680 --> 00:48:51.520
<v Aj Lawrence>No.
<v Aj Lawrence>你曾经被判过轻罪吗？

00:48:51.520 --> 00:48:54.580
<v Attorney 1>Have you ever been convicted of a misdemeanor?
<v Attorney 1>没有。

00:48:54.580 --> 00:48:55.530
<v Aj Lawrence>No.
<v Aj Lawrence>那是AJ的律师。他问AJ是怎么长大的。

00:48:55.530 --> 00:48:59.900
<v David Kestenbaum>That's AJ's attorney there. He asked AJ how he'd grown up.
<v David Kestenbaum>我有很棒的父母。妈妈和爸爸用老式的方法抚养我，尊重长辈。是的，先生。不，先生。是的，女士。不，女士。私立、预备、基督教教育，相当酷的生活。

00:48:59.900 --> 00:49:19.590
<v Aj Lawrence>I had great parents. Mom and Dad raised me the old-fashioned way, respect elders. Yes, sir. No, sir. Yes, ma'am. No, ma'am. Private, preparatory, Christian education, a pretty cool life.
<v Aj Lawrence>OK。

00:49:19.590 --> 00:49:20.520
<v Attorney>OK.
<v Attorney>AJ上过一阵子大学。他说他尝试过NBA发展联盟，但没成功。所以他成了体育教练，经常教孩子。他说家庭很喜欢他。他们对他的工作很满意，包括科迪的家人。

00:49:20.520 --> 00:49:34.240
<v David Kestenbaum>AJ went to college for a bit. He said he'd tried out for the NBA's developmental league but didn't make it. So he became an athletic trainer, often teaching kids. He said families loved him. They were really happy with his work, including Cody's family.
<v David Kestenbaum>进行得太棒了。我对他能这么快学到东西并理解，感到有些震惊，特别是他的词汇量，哇。

00:49:34.240 --> 00:49:56.680
<v Aj Lawrence>It went fantastic. I was a little bit shocked of Cody's ability to get things quickly and understand them, especially his vocabulary, wow.
<v Aj Lawrence>你开始教他的时候，他的篮球水平如何？

00:49:56.680 --> 00:50:02.820
<v Attorney 1>How were his basketball skills when you started with him?
<v Attorney 1>他会运球。我想我第一次见到他时，我对他做的第一次评估，它是——他运球就像——他能连续运球最多也就三次。他很不擅长。

00:50:02.820 --> 00:50:15.400
<v Aj Lawrence>He could dribble. I think when I first saw him, the first assessment I did with him, it was-- he dribbled like-- the most he was able to dribble consecutively was like three dribbles. He was not good.
<v Aj Lawrence>当然，他的律师问到了他的宗教信仰。

00:50:15.400 --> 00:50:19.030
<v David Kestenbaum>And, of course, his lawyer asked about his religious beliefs.
<v David Kestenbaum>你什么时候第一次了解到被提和大灾难？

00:50:19.030 --> 00:50:29.440
<v Attorney 1>When did you first learn about the rapture and the tribulation?
<v Attorney 1>抱歉，我刚才情绪化了。我只是记得沃德小姐（Miss Ward）。我真的很爱那位老师。她在六年级的中央集会上向我介绍了这个。

00:50:29.440 --> 00:50:42.670
<v Aj Lawrence>Sorry, I got emotional there. I just remember Miss Ward. I really loved that teacher. She introduced me to that in central assembly in the sixth grade.
<v Aj Lawrence>大约在AJ开始跟他谈论宗教时科迪的年纪。在审判前我心中的大问题之一是，AJ是否真的相信他告诉科迪的那些宗教内容。一种可能性是AJ会说：“是的，我们谈论过被提，光照派，RFID芯片。所有那些东西都是真的。我在试图拯救他。”

00:50:42.670 --> 00:51:03.210
<v David Kestenbaum>About the same age Cody was when AJ started talking to him about religion. One of the big questions I had going into the trial was whether AJ had actually believed all the religious stuff he'd told Cody. One possibility was that AJ would just say, yes, we talked about the rapture, the Illuminati, RFID chips. All that stuff is real. I was trying to save him.
<v David Kestenbaum>科迪指出，如果AJ真的相信这一切，为什么他不在法庭上作证？但这并非AJ所说。科迪的律师问AJ关于光照派的事。AJ说，是的，他跟科迪讨论过光照派，但不是作为统治世界的秘密社团。

00:51:03.210 --> 00:51:20.200
<v David Kestenbaum>Cody pointed out that if AJ really did believe it all, why wouldn't he just testify to that in court? But that is not what AJ said. Cody's lawyer asked AJ about the Illuminati. AJ said, yes, he had discussed the Illuminati with Cody, but not as a secret society that ran the world.
<v David Kestenbaum>我讨论它的背景是我确实相信有一些兄弟会、姐妹会会聚在一起并互相协助。而这可能就是他在互联网上看到的、被称为光照派的社团。

00:51:20.200 --> 00:51:42.430
<v Aj Lawrence>I discussed it in a context that I do believe that there are fraternities, sororities that will cling together and assist others. And that possibly could be a society that he sees on the internet, known as the Illuminati.
<v Aj Lawrence>当然，在他的Skype信息中，AJ说的是完全不同的东西。他确实似乎相信光照派。他写道——引用——“如果你不听话，光照派会杀掉你。给系统制造麻烦，你就死或者带着他们的印记生活。”

00:51:42.430 --> 00:51:56.570
<v David Kestenbaum>Of course, in his Skype messages, AJ said something very different. He really seemed to believe in the Illuminati. He wrote, quote, "The Illuminati will kill you if you don't do as you're told. Give the system problems, and you die or live with their mark."
<v David Kestenbaum>科迪的律师也问到了RFID芯片。在Skype上，AJ曾告诉科迪芯片会导致你失去思想控制——引用——“你必须知道这个芯片做了什么！！！”——三个感叹号。

00:51:56.570 --> 00:52:08.870
<v David Kestenbaum>Cody's lawyer also asked about RFID chips. Over Skype, AJ had told Cody the chips cause you to lose mind control, quote, "It's imperative that you know what this chip does!!!" - three exclamation points.
<v David Kestenbaum>但在证人席上，AJ淡化了这一点，没有说芯片是兽的印记。他谈论它们时就像它们只是他在网上读到的某种新技术。

00:52:08.870 --> 00:52:17.490
<v David Kestenbaum>But on the stand, AJ downplayed that, didn't say the chips were the mark of the beast. He talked about them like they were just some new technology he'd read about online.
<v David Kestenbaum>我在网上找到了一个链接。我相信它是北卡罗来纳大学做的。而且，还有来自加拿大阿尔伯塔的另一个链接，我相信，上面说有生物特征技术。它来了。它会储存你的信息，等等，等等，还有你所有的东西。

00:52:17.490 --> 00:52:40.980
<v Aj Lawrence>I had found a link online. I believe it was done out of the University of North Carolina. And also, there was another link out of Alberta, Canada, I believe, that said there's biometric technology. It's here. It'll store your information, et cetera, et cetera, and all your stuff.
<v Aj Lawrence>如果这被一个14岁男孩解读为与此不同的东西，你会感到惊讶吗？

00:52:40.980 --> 00:52:50.910
<v Attorney 2>Would you be surprised if this was interpreted as something different than that by a 14-year-old boy?
<v Attorney 2>在这一切之后，（轻笑）我现在并不感到惊讶。

00:52:50.910 --> 00:52:56.570
<v Aj Lawrence>After all this, [CHUCKLES] I'm not surprised now.
<v Aj Lawrence>顺便问一下，从你笑的样子来看，你认为科迪在那个容易受影响的年纪非常严肃地对待这件事，这很有趣？

00:52:56.570 --> 00:53:07.280
<v Attorney 2>I take it, by the way you're laughing about this, that you think it's funny that this was taken very seriously by Cody at the impressionable age you were communicating with him?
<v Attorney 2>不。什么——我确实非常严肃地对待这件事。我的意思是，这是一个法庭。我的意思是，辩护律师，之前我确实没怎么想过这件事，这个Skype或其他什么，它能升级到这个程度，因为这个——

00:53:07.280 --> 00:53:22.210
<v Aj Lawrence>No. What-- I do take this very seriously. I mean, this is a court of law. What I'm implying, Counsel, is before, I didn't really think nothing of this, this Skype or anything, that it can escalate to this level because this--
<v Aj Lawrence>科迪说他们谈论宗教谈了几年，AJ说他们的对话是最小限度的，加起来一共也就几小时。他说当他们谈论宗教时，是因为科迪要求的。

00:53:22.210 --> 00:53:33.010
<v David Kestenbaum>Where Cody said they had talked about religion for years, AJ said their conversations had been minimal, a matter of hours total. He said when they did talk about religion, it was because Cody had asked.
<v David Kestenbaum>关于你与科迪进行的那次诉讼主题的沟通，你觉得你有任何道歉吗？

00:53:33.010 --> 00:53:42.170
<v Attorney 3>Do you feel like you owe any apologies for the communications you had with Cody about the subject of this lawsuit?
<v Attorney 3>让我们直接跳到一切的结尾吧。我思考过这个问题。我正在看这一切，陪审团和这里的每个人，辩护律师。我真希望我能倒转时间，带特雷比格先生和太太，还有科迪去教堂多一点，这样他们就能看到，他们就能理解这个世界上还有很多其他人。有很多其他基督徒分享我对圣经的相同观点和意见。这样他们就不会把我分类或贴上我是个卑鄙或邪恶的人的标签。

00:53:42.170 --> 00:54:51.240
<v Aj Lawrence>Let's go ahead and just jump to the end of everything. I pondered on this. And I'm looking at all of this and the jury and everybody here, Counsel. And I really wish I could turn back the hands of time and taken Mr. And Mrs. Treybig, Cody to church a lot more so that they can see and they can understand that there are a lot of others in this world. There are a lot of other Christians that share my same views and opinions about the Bible. So then they wouldn't classify me or label me as being a mean or evil person.
<v Aj Lawrence>这一切都发生在奥斯汀的一个旧法庭里。它的狭小让我震惊。听众席上基本上没有人。很少有证人走上证人席——科迪、他的父母、他的心理医生和AJ。主要的实物证据是那些Skype聊天记录，长达25页。

00:54:51.240 --> 00:55:09.170
<v David Kestenbaum>This all took place in an old courtroom in Austin. I was struck by the smallness of it. There was basically no one in the audience. Very few witnesses took the stand-- Cody, his parents, his therapist, and AJ. The main piece of physical evidence was those Skype transcripts, 25 pages long.
<v David Kestenbaum>这就是我们想出来的解决人与人之间纠纷的系统。我们把一堆陌生人聚在一起，陪审团，他们只需要决定他们认为发生了什么以及它有多糟糕。科迪的律师在结案陈词中说，他们没有要求具体数额的赔偿金。但他表示科迪的心理医生费用总计超过76,000美元。而陪审团可以加上他们认为适当的精神损失赔偿。

00:55:09.170 --> 00:55:34.120
<v David Kestenbaum>This is the system we've come up with for settling disputes between people. We get a bunch of strangers together, the jury, who just have to decide what they think happened and how bad it was. Cody's lawyer, in his closing statement, said they weren't asking for a specific amount of money in damages. But he said Cody's therapist fees had totaled over $76,000. And the jury could add whatever they thought was appropriate for mental anguish.
<v David Kestenbaum>AJ的代理律师在结案陈词中说，没人能确定为什么科迪会受到如此强烈的影响。成千上万或数百万美国人分享AJ的宗教信仰，他说。为什么我们没有精神病院里塞满了害怕被提的孩子？

00:55:34.120 --> 00:55:49.380
<v David Kestenbaum>AJ's attorney, in his closing statement, said no one could know for sure why Cody was so strongly affected. Thousands or millions of Americans share AJ's religious beliefs, he said. Why don't we have mental hospitals filled with kids who are terrified of the rapture?
<v David Kestenbaum>陪审团仍然完全面无表情，周五休庭进行评议。发生的事情是否超出了所有可能的体面界限？它在文明社区中是否残暴且完全不可容忍？那天晚上，我收到了科迪的这条语音留言。

00:55:49.380 --> 00:56:05.050
<v David Kestenbaum>The jury, still totally expressionless, recessed on Friday to deliberate. Was what happened beyond all possible bounds of decency? Was it atrocious and utterly intolerable in a civilized community? That evening, I got this voicemail from Cody.
<v David Kestenbaum>嘿，大卫。我是科迪。而（叹气），哇，我们赢了。陪审团达成一致意见。我太开心了。我想唱歌。我想大喊。我想跳舞。

00:56:05.050 --> 00:56:25.220
<v Cody Treybig>Hey, David. This is Cody. And [SIGHS] wow, we won. The jury came back unanimous. And I'm just so happy. I want to sing. I want to shout. I want to dance.
<v Cody Treybig>我只是——太久以来，他是我生命中无法触及的虚构人物。（沉重地叹气）这太神奇了。我这辈子从来没这么开心过。这就是我能说的全部。

00:56:25.220 --> 00:56:44.550
<v Cody Treybig>I'm just-- for so long, he was this mythical figure in my life that I couldn't touch. [SIGHS HEAVILY] It's amazing. And I'm the happiest I've ever been in my life. And that's all I can say.
<v Cody Treybig>有一件事科迪在那条消息中没有提到——判决金额。陪审团只判给科迪4美元的赔偿，基本上是最低限度——1美元医疗，1美元精神损失，等等，这看起来很奇怪。他们同意AJ确实做了超出所有体面界限的事。但当谈到给那个数字标价时，他们的答案是4美元。

00:56:44.550 --> 00:57:10.560
<v David Kestenbaum>There is one thing Cody did not mention in that message-- the amount of the judgment. The jury awarded Cody just $4 in damages, basically the minimum-- $1 for medical care, $1 for mental anguish, et cetera, which seemed weird. They agreed that AJ had done this thing that was beyond all possible bounds of decency. But when it came time to put a dollar figure on that, their answer was $4.
<v David Kestenbaum>审判后我们跟五名陪审员谈过。所以我可以告诉你发生了什么。被选出来领导讨论、而且在现实生活中正好是一位法官的陪审长告诉我，他们以一次快速投票开始，看看每个人站在什么立场。

00:57:10.560 --> 00:57:22.680
<v David Kestenbaum>We talked to five members of the jury after the trial. So I can tell you what happened. The presiding juror who they picked to lead the discussion, and who happens to be a judge in real life, told me they started with a quick vote to see where everyone stood.
<v David Kestenbaum>七名陪审员投了赞成票，准备让AJ承担责任。三名不确定，两名反对。所以他们辩论并阅读了Skype聊天记录。然后他们进行了另一次投票，10票赞成，两票不确定，都是男性。

00:57:22.680 --> 00:57:37.930
<v David Kestenbaum>Seven jurors were yeses, ready to hold AJ liable. Three were undecided, and two were nos. So they debated and read through the Skype transcripts. And they took another vote, 10 yeses, two undecideds, both of them men.
<v David Kestenbaum>陪审长告诉我两件事似乎赢得了最后两票。一个是陪审团里的母亲们，她们直接行动起来。另一个是只判4美元赔偿的想法。每个人都喜欢那个主意。

00:57:37.930 --> 00:57:51.350
<v David Kestenbaum>The presiding juror told me two things seemed to win over the last two. One was the mothers on the jury, who just kind of went to town. And the other was this idea of awarding just $4 in damages. And everyone liked that idea.
<v David Kestenbaum>对一些人来说，这是一种承认也许AJ没意识到他正在造成的伤害的方式。在法律上，他们只需要确定他的行为是鲁莽的，不一定是故意的。一些陪审员似乎认为AJ确实相信关于RFID芯片和所有那些东西。

00:57:51.350 --> 00:58:06.570
<v David Kestenbaum>For some, it was a way to acknowledge that maybe AJ hadn't known the harm he was causing. Legally, they only had to determine that his behavior had been reckless, not necessarily intentional. It seemed to some of the jurors that AJ actually believed the stuff about RFID chips and all that.
<v David Kestenbaum>但对许多陪审员来说，4美元是一种帮助科迪将此事抛在脑后的方式。科迪的家庭不需要钱。他们想，如果他们选一个大额数字，是的，那会惩罚AJ。但他可能没能力支付，那只会让整件事拖延，更多的法律纠纷。也许AJ会上诉。所有人不得不再次经历这一切。

00:58:06.570 --> 00:58:26.690
<v David Kestenbaum>But for a lot of the jurors, the $4 was a way to help Cody put this behind him. Cody's family didn't need any money. And they figured, if they picked a large dollar amount, yes, that would punish AJ. But he probably wouldn't be able to pay it, which would just drag this whole thing out, more legal stuff. Maybe AJ would appeal. And everyone would have to go through this whole thing again.
<v David Kestenbaum>所以4美元。我不确定那是陪审团该有的想法，但他们确实这么做了。审判后，一些陪审员拥抱了科迪或走过来谈话。一个人告诉我他也试着找AJ说祝好运。但他已经离开了。

00:58:26.690 --> 00:58:43.080
<v David Kestenbaum>So $4. I'm not sure that's how the jury was supposed to think about things, but it's what they did. After the trial, some of the jurors hugged Cody or came over to talk. One told me he also tried to find AJ to say good luck. But he'd already left.
<v David Kestenbaum>我通常认为法院是决定谁进监狱，或某人欠某人多少钱的地方。在这里，它做着更基础的事情，只是说谁对了，决定记录下实际发生了什么。正如我所说，审判后，AJ终于同意跟我们谈谈了。

00:58:43.080 --> 00:59:08.180
<v David Kestenbaum>I usually think of courts as deciding who goes to prison or how much money someone owes someone else. Here, it was doing something much more basic, just saying who was right, deciding for the record what actually happened. As I said, after the trial, AJ did finally agree to talk to us.
<v David Kestenbaum>嘿，大卫。

00:59:08.180 --> 00:59:08.740
<v Aj Lawrence>Hey, David.
<v Aj Lawrence>这是AJ吗？

00:59:08.740 --> 00:59:10.660
<v David Kestenbaum>Is this AJ?
<v David Kestenbaum>是我。

00:59:10.660 --> 00:59:11.730
<v Aj Lawrence>This is he.
<v Aj Lawrence>我最感兴趣的是，这一切到底是关于什么的？他为什么要对科迪说那些话？但在我们的谈话中，AJ基本上否认说过那些话。他说科迪编造了这一切或夸大了。

00:59:11.730 --> 00:59:24.690
<v David Kestenbaum>The thing I was most interested in was, what was it all about? Why did he say all that stuff to Cody? But in our conversation, AJ basically denied saying those things. He said Cody made it all up or exaggerated.
<v David Kestenbaum>他告诉我他坚持他在法庭上说的一切，100%。而且他正计划上诉这一裁决。所以我换了一种方法。忘记他是否对科迪说了那些话。他相信那些东西吗？在法庭上不知怎么的，没人问过他那个简单的问题。

00:59:24.690 --> 00:59:41.060
<v David Kestenbaum>He told me he stood by everything he said in court, 100%. And he's planning to appeal the ruling. So I took a different approach. Forget about what he said or didn't say to Cody. Did he believe those things? Somehow in court, no one had asked him that simple question.
<v David Kestenbaum>那我可以问你关于RFID芯片吗？在法庭上，似乎你是在说你把RFID芯片看作只是某种用于储存你数据的新技术，而且你给科迪发过某条新闻报道的链接。

00:59:41.060 --> 00:59:50.900
<v David Kestenbaum>So can I ask you about RFID chips? In court, it seemed like you were saying you viewed RFID chips as just some new piece of technology to store data about you and that you'd sent Cody a link to some news story.
<v David Kestenbaum>但随后在Skype记录中，科迪写道：“它们对你的身体做了什么？”你写道：“你失去了思想控制。”而且，你还跟他进行了关于如果他父母强迫他植入RFID芯片该怎么办的完整讨论。所以到底是哪个？你认为RFID芯片只是小电脑芯片，还是你认为它们是光照派想植入每个人体内的兽的印记，你相信吗？

00:59:50.900 --> 01:00:09.770
<v David Kestenbaum>But then in the Skype transcripts, Cody writes, "What do they do to your body?" And you write, "You lose mind control." And also, you have this whole discussion with him about what to do if his parents make him get an RFID chip implanted. So which is it? Do you think RFID chips are just little computer chips or you think they're the mark of the beast that the Illuminati want to implant in everyone?
<v David Kestenbaum>你对射频有多了解？如果你在广播业工作，你应该知道很多。（轻笑）对吧？（笑着）

01:00:09.770 --> 01:00:18.850
<v Aj Lawrence>How familiar are you with radio frequency? If you're in broadcasting, you should know quite a bit. [CHUCKLES] Right? [LAUGHING]
<v Aj Lawrence>告诉我一些关于射频的事。告诉我一些在此时有用的关于射频的事。

01:00:18.850 --> 01:00:24.280
<v David Kestenbaum>Tell me something about radio frequencies. Tell me something that's useful here about radio frequencies.
<v David Kestenbaum>嗯，关于它的是你在采访我。我没在采访你。我没在这里教你关于射频的事。

01:00:24.280 --> 01:00:30.980
<v Aj Lawrence>Well, the thing about it is you're interviewing me. I'm not interviewing you. I'm not here to educate you about radio frequency.
<v Aj Lawrence>不，我只是在问个问题。你似乎是在说有什么重要的事情我应该知道，关于射频的，那会帮助我理解这一切，所以——

01:00:30.980 --> 01:00:38.320
<v David Kestenbaum>No, I'm just asking a question. You seemed to say that there's something important I should know about radio frequencies that would help me understand this, so--
<v David Kestenbaum>好吧，让我们从它们是什么开始。射频识别，就是它，OK？你口袋里可能有信用卡。它里面有个芯片。

01:00:38.320 --> 01:00:49.210
<v Aj Lawrence>Well, let's start off with what they are. Radio frequency identification is what it is, OK? You have a credit card probably in your pocket. And it has a chip in it.
<v Aj Lawrence>是的。所以这就是你认为的全部吗？你认为它也是兽的印记吗？而且是光照派想植入每个人体内并控制你思想的东西，你相信吗？

01:00:49.210 --> 01:00:57.910
<v David Kestenbaum>Yeah. So is that all you think it is? Do you think it's the mark of the beast also? And that it's something the Illuminati want to implant in everybody and will control your mind, do you believe that?
<v David Kestenbaum>嗯，首先，在法庭上，那是——我会反对那点。它叫复合提问，OK？

01:00:57.910 --> 01:01:02.590
<v Aj Lawrence>Well, first of all, in a court of law, that's-- I would object to that. It's called compounding, OK?
<v Aj Lawrence>嗯，你——

01:01:02.590 --> 01:01:02.920
<v David Kestenbaum>Well, do you--
<v David Kestenbaum>所以如果你要问我一个问题——

01:01:02.920 --> 01:01:04.690
<v Aj Lawrence>So if you're going to ask me a question--
<v Aj Lawrence>OK。

01:01:04.690 --> 01:01:04.990
<v David Kestenbaum>OK.
<v David Kestenbaum>——我的意思是，问我一个具体的。别把话塞进我嘴里，别诱导我。

01:01:04.990 --> 01:01:08.700
<v Aj Lawrence>--I mean, ask me a specific one. Don't put words in my mouth and don't lead me.
<v Aj Lawrence>OK。你相信——

01:01:08.700 --> 01:01:09.740
<v David Kestenbaum>OK. Do you believe--
<v David Kestenbaum>我真希望你别那样做。

01:01:09.740 --> 01:01:11.170
<v Aj Lawrence>I really wish you wouldn't do that.
<v Aj Lawrence>你相信RFID芯片是，或者可能是，兽的印记吗？

01:01:11.170 --> 01:01:18.240
<v David Kestenbaum>Do you believe RFID chips are, or could be, the mark of the beast?
<v David Kestenbaum>我相信它可能是一个兽的印记的潜在前传，基于它所说的，它所陈述的，在经文中。

01:01:18.240 --> 01:01:33.450
<v Aj Lawrence>I believe that it could be a potential prequel to the mark of the beast based upon what it says, what it states, in scripture.
<v Aj Lawrence>我告诉AJ专家们告诉我的，这看起来像是一个一对一邪教。他说他不是任何邪教的一部分。

01:01:33.450 --> 01:01:41.360
<v David Kestenbaum>I told AJ what the experts told me, that this seemed like a one-on-one cult. He said he's not part of any cult.
<v David Kestenbaum>你现在怎么看科迪？

01:01:41.360 --> 01:01:44.540
<v David Kestenbaum>How do you feel about Cody now?
<v David Kestenbaum>阐述一下。

01:01:44.540 --> 01:01:47.040
<v Aj Lawrence>Elaborate.
<v Aj Lawrence>我不知道。我的意思是，你在生他的气吗？

01:01:47.040 --> 01:01:50.820
<v David Kestenbaum>I don't know. I mean, are you angry at him?
<v David Kestenbaum>我为他感到难过。我为他感到难过。我真的是。我很失望。我很悲伤。但你知道吗？生活是关于选择的。所以在审判日，他将不得不站在父亲面前。而那是——复仇是属于父亲的。

01:01:50.820 --> 01:02:14.680
<v Aj Lawrence>I feel sorry for him. I feel sorry for him. I really do. I'm really disappointed. I'm sad. But you know what? Life is about choices. So on Judgment Day, he'll have to stand before the father. And that's-- vengeance is that of the father.
<v Aj Lawrence>如果你问我听了四天的证词，阅读审判文件，采访家庭和其他孩子，以及AJ两个小时后，我对这一切有什么看法，我认为AJ相信所有他告诉科迪的东西。我找到了一个在这一切之前就认识AJ的人，他说他一直充满热情地谈论RFID芯片和光照派。

01:02:14.680 --> 01:02:35.750
<v David Kestenbaum>If you ask me what I make of all this after listening to four days of testimony, reading through the trial documents, interviewing the family and other kids and AJ for two hours, I think AJ believes all the stuff he told Cody. I found someone who knew AJ before all this, who said he talked about RFID chips and the Illuminati all the time, with enthusiasm.
<v David Kestenbaum>我认为AJ的诉求和科迪的一样。这是一个他身处中心的故事。我认为他学会了如何以一种吸引人们进入的方式展示它。而科迪是一个崇拜AJ的甜美孩子。

01:02:35.750 --> 01:02:51.750
<v David Kestenbaum>I think the appeal for AJ is the same as it was for Cody. It's a story he is at the center of. I think he's learned how to present it in a way that draws people in. And Cody was a sweet kid who adored AJ.
<v David Kestenbaum>审判后我去拜访了科迪。看起来审判案件确实把他这件事的最后一部分抛在了身后。就像科迪克服了他对被提和光照派的恐惧。但他仍然有一个更普通的恐惧。他害怕一个人。但在法庭上，AJ看起来并不超自然。他看起来非常像个人。

01:02:51.750 --> 01:03:11.570
<v David Kestenbaum>I went to visit Cody after the trial. It did seem like the court case had put some final piece of this thing behind him. It was like Cody had gotten over his fear of the rapture and the Illuminati. But he still had a much more ordinary fear. He was afraid of a person. But in court, AJ did not seem supernatural. He seemed very human.
<v David Kestenbaum>你看着他，你说，你只是个男人。你只是个人。

01:03:11.570 --> 01:03:18.010
<v Cody Treybig>You look at him, and you say, you're just a guy. You're just a person.
<v Cody Treybig>这件事之所以对你如此有影响力，是不是因为你活在那个关于世界是如何运作的完全不同的版本里太久了。而在此时，你终于决定了什么是真实的。现在你把它展示给了一群别人，在我们要决定什么是真实、发生了什么的设定中。他们说了“是的”。

01:03:18.010 --> 01:03:42.100
<v David Kestenbaum>Is part of why this was so powerful for you that you lived for so long with this totally other version of how the world was. And here's the time where you've finally decided what is real. And now you've shown it to a bunch of other people in this very setting where we are supposed to decide what is real and what happened. And they said yes.
<v David Kestenbaum>正是如此。你回头看，你简直无法相信你说出的那些事真的发生了。对我来说，那现在是疯狂的事。那是疯狂的事。而它发生在你身上，这很奇怪。

01:03:42.100 --> 01:04:02.580
<v Cody Treybig>That's exactly it. You look back, and you just can't believe that the things you're saying actually happened. It's crazy stuff to me now. It's crazy stuff. And that it happened to you, it's weird.
<v Cody Treybig>我认为那是苍蝇最终从外面看着瓶子时说的话。

01:04:02.580 --> 01:04:14.260
<v David Kestenbaum>I think those are the words of a fly finally looking at the bottle from the outside.
<v David Kestenbaum>大卫·凯斯滕鲍姆，他是我们节目的制作人之一。

01:04:14.260 --> 01:04:17.520
<v Ira Glass>David Kestenbaum, he's one of the producers of our show.
<v Ira Glass>（音乐——Living Color的《Cult of Personality》）

